/* nbh-video-transcripts.js (v25.72): the transcript of each procedure video, for its page in the video library
   (videos.html: the Transcript tab beside the Steps, and the captions turned on with CC). AI-generated videos of simulated
   people; nothing here is about a student. Per video, in time order: [start in seconds, who, text], who 'N' the narrator,
   'O' on screen (a caption or a card point the narration does not say: what the picture shows), or the role of the
   person speaking (Teacher, Student, Aide, Peer, Classmate, Instructor). The narration is the script the voice read; the
   people's lines are the captions' quotes, each checked against the sound with a speech recognizer on this computer, and
   timed where it was heard. A card's notes start with the card. */
window.NBH_VIDEO_TRANSCRIPTS = {
 "dro_resetting": [[0.0,"O","Restart the interval from zero each time problem behavior occurs (step 5)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Kodak, T., Miltenberger, R. G., & Romaniuk, C. (2003). The effects of differential negative reinforcement of other behavior and noncontingent escape on compliance. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 36(3), 379–382."],[0.6,"N","Differential reinforcement of other behavior, resetting: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Kodak and colleagues, 2003."],[12.6,"N","First, confirm that problem behavior is maintained by escape: it happens most during work, and it is followed by a break from the task."],[22.0,"N","In baseline, find the mean time between problem behaviors: session minutes divided by the number of responses. Start the interval below it."],[32.3,"O","The first interval is set below the baseline mean time between problem behaviors: here, 10 seconds."],[32.6,"N","During work, each correct answer gets praise, and a timer runs. Here the interval starts at ten seconds."],[40.1,"Teacher","Put the red one in."],[42.8,"Teacher","Nice."],[42.8,"O","The timer runs."],[44.5,"Teacher","You worked so calmly. Break time!"],[53.0,"N","Each interval with no problem behavior earns a short break from the work, starting within two seconds of the beep."],[60.1,"O","The break ends: back to the table, a new interval, and the next instruction. Prompts go words first, then a model (step 6)."],[60.4,"N","When the break ends, she restarts the timer and gives the next instruction. If he doesn't start, she prompts with words first, then a model."],[69.9,"Teacher","Break is over. Come sit, please."],[69.9,"O","She restarts the timer at 0:10."],[72.7,"Teacher","Put the yellow one in."],[72.7,"O","He doesn’t start."],[76.3,"Teacher","Watch."],[76.3,"O","A model, then the shape in his hand."],[77.4,"Teacher","Good job."],[83.3,"N","In another interval, problem behavior happens. The interval restarts from zero, with no comment, and the work goes on."],[91.9,"Teacher","Put the blue one in."],[94.0,"Student","No!"],[94.0,"O","She restarts the timer without comment and gives the request again."],[95.2,"Teacher","Put the blue one in."],[99.0,"Teacher","Good job."],[102.5,"O","When problem behavior is at or below the criterion, lengthen the interval: 10 s, 20 s, 30 s, 1 min, 1.5 min, then 2 min (step 8)."],[102.5,"O","If it has not dropped after about 7 sessions, lengthen each break (step 9)."],[102.5,"O","Check fidelity in a sample of sessions: retrain staff below 80%; DRO mostly stopped working at 40% or less (O’Neill et al., 2026). Record which other behavior comes just before each reinforcer, and bring any other problem behavior that rises to the team (Jessel et al., 2015)."],[103.1,"N","When problem behavior is at or below the criterion, lengthen the interval, step by step, to two minutes."],[110.9,"N","Physical guidance is a restrictive step. Use it only with consent and the plan's approval."],[117.4,"N","Check in a sample of sessions how often staff run the DRO as written, and retrain below eighty percent. And watch which other behavior the reinforcer is strengthening."],[129.0,"N","Check that each earned break starts on time. Adapt every step to the student’s plan."]],
 "dro_class": [[0.0,"O","A treat for each interval with no disruption; later, a check only at the moment it ends."],[0.0,"O","Check the treat first: it should raise a simple task response by 25% or more (Repp et al., step 1). Pair it with praise (Barton et al., step 2)."],[0.0,"O","Interval: the baseline mean time between responses, rounded (Barton et al.); Repp et al. used 5 min."],[0.0,"O","These studies did not reset the timer after disruption; most practice does (see the resetting video)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Repp, A. C., Barton, L. E., & Brulle, A. R. (1983). A comparison of two procedures for programming the differential reinforcement of other behaviors. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 16(4), 435–445. Barton, L. E., Brulle, A. R., & Repp, A. C. (1986). Maintenance of therapeutic change by momentary DRO. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 19(3), 277–282."],[0.6,"N","DRO in a classroom, whole-interval, then momentary: a simulated example of the TD-1 cards after Repp and colleagues, 1983, and Barton and colleagues, 1986."],[16.0,"N","Check the treat first: it should work as a reinforcer on an easy task. Pair each treat with praise."],[23.6,"N","Barton and colleagues set the interval at the baseline mean time between responses. In these studies the timer was not reset after disruption."],[33.4,"O","The aide sits beside him and times each interval."],[33.7,"N","The aide times each interval. A whole interval with no disruption earns a treat, given without stopping the lesson."],[42.1,"O","At the start of the session, tell the student the rule (Repp et al., step 2)."],[42.4,"N","At the start of the session, tell the student the rule: no disruption for the whole five minutes earns a treat. Then start the timer."],[51.7,"Aide","If you stay on task the whole five minutes, you earn a cracker."],[51.7,"O","He nods and works; she starts the timer."],[59.1,"O","No disruption in the whole interval: the treat."],[64.0,"Aide","Nice job working."],[64.0,"O","The lesson goes on."],[69.1,"O","Another interval."],[69.4,"N","Disruption during an interval gets no comment, and the timer keeps running. When that interval ends, nothing is given."],[78,"O","He turns and talks to a classmate. No comment; the timer runs on. When it ends, nothing is given."],[79.8,"Student","Hey, look at this!"],[90.3,"N","Once disruption is low and stable, switch to momentary checks: only the moment the interval ends counts."],[98.3,"O","He looks away for a moment, but is working when the timer vibrates: the treat."],[98.3,"Aide","Nice working."],[109,"O","Check fidelity in a sample of sessions: retrain staff below 80%; DRO mostly stopped working at 40% or less (O’Neill et al., 2026). Record which other behavior comes just before each reinforcer, and bring any other problem behavior that rises to the team (Jessel et al., 2015)."],[109.6,"N","If disruption rises again, go back to whole-interval DRO."],[115.1,"N","If staff can't watch every whole interval, alternate whole-interval and momentary periods, each signaled by its own desk-card color."],[124.2,"N","Check in a sample of sessions how often staff run the DRO as written, and retrain below eighty percent. And watch which other behavior the reinforcer is strengthening."],[135.8,"N","Keep a DRO schedule in place rather than stopping it. Adapt every step to the student’s plan."]],
 "dro_momentary": [[0.0,"O","The item comes only if the behavior is not happening at the second the interval ends."],[0.0,"O","Confirm the function and use that reinforcer: the item, or 3–5 s of talk for attention (step 1). Do not explain the rule (Hammond et al., step 3)."],[0.0,"O","Give no sign an interval is ending: do not reach for or look at the item early."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Hammond, J. L., Iwata, B. A., Fritz, J. N., & Dempsey, C. M. (2011). Evaluation of fixed momentary DRO schedules under signaled and unsignaled arrangements. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 44(1), 69–81. Lindberg, J. S., Iwata, B. A., Kahng, S., & DeLeon, I. G. (1999). DRO contingencies: An analysis of variable-momentary schedules. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 32(2), 123–136."],[0.6,"N","Momentary DRO, fixed and variable: a simulated example of the TD-1 cards after Hammond and colleagues, 2011, and Lindberg and colleagues, 1999."],[15.6,"N","Confirm the function first, and use that reinforcer. Don't explain the rule to the student."],[22.3,"N","The first interval is the mean time between responses over the last three baseline sessions."],[29.0,"N","The tablet is close but out of sight. A silent timer runs, and he gets no sign that an interval is ending."],[36.9,"O","At the second the interval ends he is sorting calmly: the tablet, right away."],[44.6,"Teacher","There you go."],[47,"O","Access is short: 30 s with toys or a video, or one small edible (Hammond et al., step 5)."],[47.3,"N","Access is short: thirty seconds with toys or a video, or one small edible. Then the item goes away and the next interval starts."],[60.1,"O","Variable momentary DRO: intervals of 50%, 75%, 100%, 125% and 150% of the mean, in random order, each used equally often (Lindberg et al., step 3)."],[60.4,"N","In variable momentary DRO, the checks come at unpredictable times: from half the mean to one and a half times the mean, in random order."],[64.7,"Teacher","Time’s up."],[64.7,"O","The tablet goes back on the shelf, and the next interval starts."],[70.6,"O","This interval is half the mean. He is sorting calmly at the second it ends: the teacher reaches for the tablet at once."],[75.1,"Teacher","Here you go."],[75.1,"O","Keep the item within one reach, so the hand-over starts the second the interval ends."],[78.2,"O","Another interval. Problem behavior during it gets no response, and the interval runs to its end, with no reset (Lindberg et al., step 6)."],[78.5,"N","At the end of another interval he is slapping the table. Nothing is given, and nothing else is said."],[85.6,"N","Problem behavior in the middle of an interval doesn't restart it. The interval runs to its end."],[92.7,"Student","Give it!"],[92.7,"O","He is slapping the table as the interval ends: no item, no response. The next interval starts."],[102.1,"O","Fixed: after 2 consecutive sessions at least 80% below baseline, lengthen the interval by 50%, rounded to the nearest 5 s, up to 5 min (Hammond et al., step 7)."],[102.1,"O","Variable: set each session’s mean interval from the mean time between responses in the last 3 sessions, never more than doubling it; hold a terminal interval (300 s in the study) while behavior stays below 0.5 per minute (Lindberg et al., steps 7–8)."],[102.1,"O","If behavior stops just before the item comes, look for staff cues the student may be reading, and remove them (Hammond et al., step 8)."],[102.1,"O","Check fidelity in a sample of sessions: retrain staff below 80%; DRO mostly stopped working at 40% or less (O’Neill et al., 2026). Record which other behavior comes just before each reinforcer, and bring any other problem behavior that rises to the team (Jessel et al., 2015)."],[102.7,"N","After two good sessions, lengthen the interval by half, up to five minutes."],[108.3,"N","On a variable schedule, set each session's mean interval from the last three sessions, never more than doubling it."],[116.4,"N","Check in a sample of sessions how often staff run the DRO as written, and retrain below eighty percent. And watch which other behavior the reinforcer is strengthening."],[128.0,"N","Teach a request for the same item alongside DRO, and adapt every step to the student’s plan."]],
 "dro_tokens": [[0.0,"O","Confirm the behavior is not maintained by attention, escape or items (Tiger et al., step 1)."],[0.0,"O","Teach her to notice each instance, and a competing response: hands in the lap (steps 2–3)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Tiger, J. H., Fisher, W. W., & Bouxsein, K. J. (2009). Therapist- and self-monitored DRO contingencies as a treatment for the self-injurious skin picking of a young man with Asperger syndrome. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 42(2), 315–319. Tereshko, L., Ross, R. K., & Frazee, L. (2021). The effects of a procedure to decrease motor stereotypy on social interactions in a child with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 14(2), 367–377."],[0.6,"N","DRO with tokens, then student-run: a simulated example of the TD-1 cards after Tiger and colleagues, 2009, and Tereshko and colleagues, 2021."],[15.1,"N","First, teach the student to notice the behavior, and to put her hands in her lap instead. Then explain the rule: a ticket for each interval without picking, traded later for a small reward."],[28.4,"O","The rule was explained before the session, with no prompts during it (Tiger et al., steps 4–5)."],[28.7,"N","Staff time each interval. When it ends with no picking, she gets praise and a ticket, and the timer starts again."],[36.9,"O","The timer beeps with no picking."],[41.1,"Teacher","Five minutes with your hands down. Here’s a ticket."],[41.1,"O","She restarts the timer."],[46.9,"O","Another interval."],[47.2,"N","If picking happens, staff restart the timer without a word. There are no prompts during the session."],[54.3,"O","She picks at her thumb. The aide restarts the timer without a word, and the girl puts her hands in her lap."],[61.8,"O","Weeks later, with picking near zero: 10-min intervals, and she has been taught to set and reset the timer herself (Tiger et al., steps 7–8)."],[62.1,"N","Once picking stays near zero, lengthen the interval. Then teach the student to run the timer and the tickets herself."],[70.6,"O","Staff step out of view. The timer beeps: she puts a ticket in her own envelope and restarts it at 10:00."],[82.6,"O","Staff observe out of view and compare her record with their own data each session (Tiger et al., steps 9–10)."],[82.9,"N","Staff step out of view, and compare the student's record with their own data."],[87.9,"O","Lengthen when the behavior stays near zero: 5, 10, then 15 min (Tiger et al.), or 1 min longer after 2 days at zero, then the student chooses (Tereshko et al., step 6)."],[87.9,"O","Fade the timer and board to a vibrating watch with the same rules, and carry the routine to new places and people (Tereshko et al., steps 7–8)."],[87.9,"O","Check fidelity in a sample of sessions: retrain staff below 80%; DRO mostly stopped working at 40% or less (O’Neill et al., 2026). Record which other behavior comes just before each reinforcer, and bring any other problem behavior that rises to the team (Jessel et al., 2015)."],[87.9,"O","Each session, check her record against staff data: resets after picking, a ticket at the beep, the timer restarted (Tiger et al., step 10). Adapt every step to the student’s plan."],[88.5,"N","Lengthen the interval step by step, and fade the timer and board to a vibrating watch with the same rules."],[95.8,"N","Check in a sample of sessions how often staff run the DRO as written, and retrain below eighty percent. And watch which other behavior the reinforcer is strengthening."],[107.4,"N","Check the student's record each session, and adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "dra_attention": [[0.0,"O","Pick a daily 30-minute seatwork period and define study behavior for it, such as pencil on paper (step 1)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Hall, R. V., Lund, D., & Jackson, D. (1968). Effects of teacher attention on study behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 1(1), 1–12."],[0.6,"N","Differential reinforcement of alternative behavior, with teacher attention: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Hall, Lund and Jackson, 1968."],[13.8,"N","First, record study behavior in seatwork for about two weeks, and note whether your attention follows work or off-task behavior. Check that the work is at the student's level."],[25.7,"O","He looks out of the window, not working. She keeps helping another student at the back."],[26.0,"N","While he is off task, there are no reminders, no urging and no scolding. When he starts to work, she goes to him right away and praises the work. At first, even first steps, like picking up the pencil, earn praise."],[41.9,"O","No look, no reminder, no scolding. She keeps helping the group."],[47,"O","He picks up the pencil and starts to write. Right away."],[51.5,"Teacher","Look at you working. Nice job on number two."],[54.2,"O","Brief, then back to the group, and he keeps writing."],[56.8,"O","A few minutes later. An observer comes to the back of the room with a clipboard."],[57.3,"N","Then attend after each minute of steady work. An observer can cue the teacher by quietly raising a colored card. A short comment on the work, or a light pat on the shoulder, is enough."],[69.9,"O","He has worked for a minute without a break: the observer quietly raises a green card."],[76.6,"Teacher","You kept working!"],[76.6,"O","A light pat on the shoulder, and a word about his work."],[78.6,"O","Brief, then back to the group."],[83.1,"O","Later in the period."],[83.9,"N","If he leaves his seat to get help, she does not respond. When he raises his hand while seated, she answers right away."],[91.6,"O","He leaves his seat and holds out his workbook: no look and no answer. He goes back to his desk."],[99.7,"O","Seated, he raises his hand: she comes over at once and answers briefly."],[102.6,"Teacher","Good question. Start with this one."],[107,"O","No functional analysis was done in the study: it fits mild off-task behavior that attention seems to maintain. One teacher needed more coaching to shift her attention."],[108.1,"N","Once study stays high, drop the cue, and keep praising work all through the day. Review the graph with the teacher each week."],[116.8,"N","Check in a sample of sessions that attention follows work and not off-task behavior, and coach the teacher when it slips. Working has to pay off better than being off task."],[128.4,"N","Planned ignoring is the extinction part. Use it only for behavior that is safe to ignore, and step in for anything unsafe. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "dra_escape": [[0.0,"O","Problem behavior still ends the task, but doing the task pays off better: a longer break, with favorite things and attention."],[0.0,"O","Find the student’s top kind of attention by offering pictures in pairs, and rank leisure items; snacks only if the team approves (step 2)."],[0.0,"O","Give instructions through each 10-minute session: say it, then show it (step 3)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Briggs, A. M., Dozier, C. L., Lessor, A. N., Kamana, B. U., & Jess, R. L. (2019). Further investigation of differential reinforcement of alternative behavior without extinction for escape-maintained destructive behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 52(4), 956–973."],[0.6,"N","Differential reinforcement of alternative behavior for escape, without extinction: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Briggs and colleagues, 2019."],[13.5,"N","First, confirm that problem behavior happens to escape instructions. Then find the student's favorite kind of attention and her top leisure items, with preference checks."],[25.1,"O","The worksheet is in front of her; her favorite things are in a bin on the shelf behind."],[25.3,"N","Give instructions through the session, with a spoken prompt, then a model. When she does the task, praise her, take the work away, and start a two-minute break with her favorite things and his full attention."],[39.2,"Teacher","Circle the biggest number."],[41.2,"O","She does it: she circles the biggest number and puts the pencil down."],[44.6,"Teacher","You did it! Break time."],[46.8,"O","The work goes away. Two minutes with her favorite things, and with his full attention."],[56.7,"Teacher","Wow! You’re doing a great job. Look at those colors!"],[60.5,"O","Another trial."],[61.0,"N","If problem behavior happens, the work goes away too, but the break is short and plain: thirty seconds, with no attention and no items. Then the instructions start again."],[72.9,"Teacher","Circle the next one."],[74.4,"O","She pushes the work away. Without a word, he takes the work away."],[78.7,"O","A short, plain break: 30 seconds (shortened here), with no talk and no favorites. Then the next instruction."],[85.1,"Teacher","Circle the next one."],[88.2,"N","Once problem behavior is low and steady, each task earns a token, and a full board earns the better break. Raise the number of tokens slowly, toward twenty."],[99.2,"O","She does the task, and he hands her a star token."],[101.2,"Teacher","You filled your board!"],[103.2,"O","The last star fills her board."],[105.7,"O","Board full: a high five, and the better break comes next, as in step 3."],[107.2,"O","If problem behavior stays high, lengthen the better break (step 6). Use attention the student welcomes, and snacks only if the team approves."],[108.4,"N","Raise it after two sessions with problem behavior at least ninety percent below the start. If it rises for two sessions, go back a step."],[117.8,"N","Without extinction, the alternative has to pay off better than problem behavior. In this study, a longer break alone, or favorite things alone, did not hold. Both were needed. Check in a sample of sessions that every break is the right kind and length."],[135.0,"N","Physical help after the model is a restrictive step: stop if the student pulls away, and plan it with consent. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "dra_tangible": [[0.0,"O","Ending the activity when asked earns a coupon at once, and each coupon buys uninterrupted time with it later."],[0.0,"O","For a young student or one with autism, teach the trade first with one coupon traded at once, with a picture of what it buys."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Wilder, D. A., Harris, C., Reagan, R., & Rasey, A. (2007). Functional analysis and treatment of noncompliance by preschool children. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 40(1), 173–177."],[0.6,"N","Differential reinforcement of alternative behavior for ending a favorite activity: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Wilder and colleagues, 2007."],[13.4,"N","First, check with brief trials that the student mainly does not comply when told to end a favorite activity. Before the first session, explain the coupon trade, and model earning and trading a coupon."],[26.7,"O","She is watching a favorite video. A small stack of coupons and an empty cup are on the table."],[27.0,"N","During the favorite activity, give one instruction to end it: turn off the video. If she starts within ten seconds, say thank you, and hand over a coupon right away."],[39.1,"Teacher","Turn off the video, please."],[39.1,"O","One instruction, with a point."],[47.5,"Teacher","Thank you! Here’s a coupon."],[47.5,"O","The coupon comes right away, and she keeps it to trade later."],[53.3,"O","Another trial."],[53.6,"N","If she does not comply within ten seconds, there is no coupon and no comment. The instruction is not repeated. Wait for the next planned opportunity."],[65.3,"Teacher","Turn off the video, please."],[65.3,"O","One instruction, then ten seconds with no response: no coupon, no comment, no repeat. She keeps the video for now, and the teacher turns to other work."],[80.0,"N","At the end of the session, she trades her coupons. Each one buys three minutes with the video, with no interruptions."],[87.8,"O","She trades her coupons: three coupons, nine minutes of iPad time."],[93.5,"O","Time with the video, with no interruptions."],[100.4,"O","The evidence is two preschoolers and one instruction; the classroom version was not evaluated."],[101.3,"N","In class, give coupons for the hardest instructions, each worth a minute of the favorite activity at a time that suits the schedule. Record compliance for every instruction."],[112.8,"N","This is DRA without extinction. Not complying still kept the video, but complying paid off better: a coupon right away, and time that would not be interrupted. Check in a sample of sessions that every coupon earned is handed over and traded."],[130.5,"N","The study did not thin the coupons, so plan that step. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "dra_automatic": [[0.0,"O","Play with leisure materials earns short periods of free stereotypy; the play needed grows."],[0.0,"O","Pick 3 or 4 age-fitting materials, kept for these sessions (step 2)."],[0.0,"O","Start at 1 response (Potter et al.) or 5 s of handling (Hanley et al.)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Potter, J. N., Hanley, G. P., Augustine, M., Clay, C. J., & Phelps, M. C. (2013). Treating stereotypy in adolescents diagnosed with autism by refining the tactic of “using stereotypy as reinforcement.” Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 46(2), 407–423. Hanley, G. P., Iwata, B. A., Thompson, R. H., & Lindberg, J. S. (2000). A component analysis of “stereotypy as reinforcement” for alternative behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 33(3), 285–297."],[0.6,"N","Differential reinforcement of alternative behavior for automatically maintained stereotypy: a simulated example of the TD-1 cards after Potter and colleagues, 2013, and Hanley and colleagues, 2000."],[16.3,"N","Confirm that the stereotypy continues when the student is alone, and that leisure items alone do not compete with it. Keep a few age-fitting materials for these sessions only."],[28.1,"O","He rubs and flicks his fingers in front of his chest. The bricks are on the table."],[28.4,"N","Seat the student with the materials. If he is not using one, prompt every thirty seconds: a model, a point, or a word."],[37.6,"O","He is not using the materials: the aide adds a brick (a model), then points at the pile."],[49.8,"O","He adds one more brick: the requirement is met, with no stereotypy."],[50.2,"N","When he meets the requirement without stereotypy, say the break is earned, turn his chair back from the table, and step about a meter away. For thirty seconds he is free, with no prompts and no blocking."],[65.7,"Teacher","You earned a break!"],[67.3,"O","The aide steps about a meter back and the chair turns away from the table: 30 seconds of free access, with no prompts and no blocking."],[75.9,"N","When the thirty seconds end, turn the chair back, and the work time starts again."],[82.3,"O","Time up: the chair turns back to the bricks, and the next requirement starts."],[95,"O","Run 5- to 10-minute sessions and record stereotypy at the table and during breaks separately. The effects did not carry over without the contingency."],[95.9,"N","Raise the requirement one step at a time, after several sessions with low stereotypy and steady play. Later, count only building steps toward a finished product."],[107.5,"N","Playing has to pay off better than stereotypy at the table, so honor every earned break, on time and in full, and check this in a sample of sessions."],[118.5,"N","These studies also blocked stereotypy at the table. Blocking is a restrictive step, not shown here: it needs district review and written consent, and is used only with the earned breaks."],[131.5,"N","Allow only forms of stereotypy that are safe during the break. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "ncr_attention": [[0.0,"O","Attention by the clock, so calling out is not needed."],[0.0,"O","Confirm that adult attention follows most disruptions (Rasmussen & O’Neill, step 1)."],[0.0,"O","Set the interval from the baseline rate of attention (Noel & Getch: 1 min) or the mean time to the first disruption (Rasmussen & O’Neill: 10–20 s)."],[0.0,"O","At each cue, a brief neutral comment by name within 5 s, whatever the student is doing (Noel & Getch, steps 4–5)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Noel, C. R., & Getch, Y. Q. (2016). Noncontingent reinforcement in after-school settings to decrease classroom disruptive behavior for students with autism spectrum disorder. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(3), 261–265. Rasmussen, K., & O'Neill, R. E. (2006). The effects of fixed-time reinforcement schedules on problem behavior of children with emotional and behavioral disorders in a day-treatment classroom setting. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 39(4), 453–457."],[0.6,"N","Noncontingent reinforcement for attention in class: a simulated example of the TD-1 cards after Noel and Getch, 2016, and Rasmussen and O'Neill, 2006."],[14.5,"N","Confirm that adult attention maintains the behavior. Set the interval from baseline, then give brief attention on a timer, whatever the student is doing."],[25,"O","His watch buzzes every minute while he teaches the group."],[25.3,"N","A vibrating watch cues the teacher. At each buzz he gives the student a short comment by name, then goes back to the group."],[34.1,"O","At the buzz."],[39.6,"Teacher","Arjun, I see you found the page."],[39.6,"O","Then straight back to the group."],[44.5,"N","Between cues, calling out gets no response. A quiet raised hand is answered quickly and briefly."],[53.6,"Student","Mr. Ramirez!"],[55.1,"Student","Mr. Ramirez! Look!"],[55.1,"O","No look and no answer; he keeps helping another student."],[57.2,"O","A quiet raised hand."],[59.9,"Teacher","Yes, Arjun?"],[59.9,"O","A brief answer, then back to the group (Rasmussen & O’Neill, step 6)."],[62.0,"Teacher","Good, keep reading."],[64.7,"O","Thin in steps once the behavior stays low, never cutting the rate by more than a third at once; go back a step if it rises (Hagopian et al., 1994, steps 5–6)."],[64.7,"O","If a cue lands during the behavior: most cards deliver anyway; Rasmussen & O’Neill waited 10 s (step 5). Choose one with the BCBA and write it in the plan."],[64.7,"O","With a teacher who can manage only a lean schedule, set it together (Austin & Soeda, 2008: every 4 min). A greeting by name at the door gives attention first (Allday & Pakurar, 2007)."],[64.7,"O","Mark every scheduled delivery as given or missed, and retrain staff at once after a lapse: at 80% integrity, fixed schedules lost the effect for most people (Jones et al., 2025)."],[65.3,"N","Thin the schedule step by step once the behavior stays low, and go back a step if it rises."],[72.2,"N","Mark every scheduled delivery as given or missed, and retrain staff right away after a lapse. Missed deliveries can undo the effect."],[81.8,"N","A greeting by name at the door is another way to give attention first. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "ncr_items": [[0.0,"O","The item comes by the clock, so problem behavior is not needed to get it."],[0.0,"O","Confirm the item function with a functional analysis (Lalli et al., step 1)."],[0.0,"O","First interval: the mean time without problem behavior after the item is removed in baseline (the study: 90–120 s), or the mean time between responses (Kahng et al., 2000a)."],[0.0,"O","Tell the student the item comes when the timer rings, and offer other toys first (Lalli et al., step 3)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Lalli, J. S., Casey, S. D., & Kates, K. (1997). Noncontingent reinforcement as treatment for severe problem behavior: Some procedural variations. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 30(1), 127–137. Goh, H.-L., Iwata, B. A., & DeLeon, I. G. (2000). Competition between noncontingent and contingent reinforcement schedules during response acquisition. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 33(2), 195–205."],[0.6,"N","Noncontingent reinforcement for items: a simulated example of the TD-1 cards after Lalli and colleagues, 1997, and Goh and colleagues, 2000."],[13.7,"N","Set the first interval from baseline: how long the student goes without problem behavior after the item is taken away."],[21.8,"O","Here the interval is 2 min. She plays with blocks while it runs."],[22.1,"N","When the timer rings, the item comes for a set time, whatever she is doing."],[27.5,"O","The timer rings."],[32.3,"Teacher","Here’s your music!"],[32.3,"O","30 s with it, whatever she is doing at that moment."],[37.5,"O","When the access time ends (Gouboth et al., steps 5–7)."],[37.8,"N","Before the item goes, say when it comes back, and set a timer she can see. Problem behavior gets no item and no reaction."],[47.1,"O","She sets the timer where the girl can see it."],[50.0,"Teacher","The music comes back when the timer rings."],[50.1,"O","She reaches and whines: no music and no reaction. Blocks are offered, and the timer keeps running."],[57.2,"O","Teach a request for the same item alongside the schedule (Goh et al., steps 2–4)."],[57.5,"N","Teach a request for the same item alongside the schedule, and honor every request."],[63.4,"O","She hands over the picture card."],[65.8,"Teacher","You asked! Here you go."],[65.8,"O","Every request is honored."],[72,"O","Lengthen after 2–3 sessions with no problem behavior; shorten if it stays at baseline for 3 sessions (Lalli et al., steps 6–7). Or set each session from the last 3 sessions’ mean time between responses, never more than doubling it (Kahng et al., 2000a)."],[72,"O","A variable schedule, 50% below to 50% above the mean, also works (Van Camp et al., 2000b). Give a large enough amount each time (Carr et al., 1998)."],[72,"O","If the behavior starts within seconds of removal, NCR alone may fail: teach the request early (Fritz et al., 2017a). Keep extinction in place when deliveries lapse (Saini et al., 2017)."],[72,"O","Mark every scheduled delivery as given or missed, and retrain staff at once after a lapse: at 80% integrity, fixed schedules lost the effect for most people (Jones et al., 2025)."],[72.6,"N","Lengthen the interval after two or three sessions without problem behavior, and shorten it if the behavior returns."],[80.2,"N","Mark every scheduled delivery as given or missed, and retrain staff right away after a lapse. Missed deliveries can undo the effect."],[89.8,"N","If the behavior starts within seconds of the item going away, add the request early. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "ncr_work": [[0.0,"O","The good things come by the clock while the work goes on, so problem behavior is not needed to get them."],[0.0,"O","Confirm the escape function, and revise tasks that are too hard or too long first (Geiger et al., 2010, step 2)."],[0.0,"O","Snacks: a list of variable intervals averaging 15 s; at each signal one piece and a comment unrelated to the task, whatever the student is doing (Lomas et al., step 5)."],[0.0,"O","Breaks (NCE): the first interval at the mean time to the first disruption in baseline, cued by work and break notes (Waller & Higbee, steps 2–6)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Lomas, J. E., Fisher, W. W., & Kelley, M. E. (2010). The effects of variable-time delivery of food items and praise on problem behavior reinforced by escape. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 43(3), 425–435. Waller, R. D., & Higbee, T. S. (2010). The effects of fixed-time escape on inappropriate and appropriate classroom behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 43(1), 149–153."],[0.6,"N","Noncontingent reinforcement during work: a simulated example of the TD-1 cards after Lomas and colleagues, 2010, and Waller and Higbee, 2010."],[13.3,"N","For behavior maintained by escape from work, give a preferred snack, or a short break, on a timer, while the work goes on."],[21.8,"O","Her watch vibrates on a variable schedule averaging 15 s."],[22.1,"N","On a variable schedule, about every fifteen seconds, he gets a small snack and a friendly comment unrelated to the work. Then the next task."],[32,"O","At the buzz: one cracker."],[36.0,"Teacher","You’re a cool kid."],[36.0,"O","Whatever he is doing."],[39.6,"Teacher","Next one."],[39.6,"O","The pace of tasks stays the same as before (Lomas et al., step 8)."],[42,"O","Another way: noncontingent escape. Breaks come by the clock, never after problem behavior (Waller & Higbee, step 9)."],[42.3,"N","Or give breaks by the clock. The timer cues the break note, whatever he is doing. Then the work note, and work resumes."],[51.6,"O","The silent timer buzzes; she taps the orange break note. He stretches in his seat."],[59.3,"O","The timer buzzes again; she taps the yellow work note, and he picks up his pencil."],[64.1,"O","Lengthen the break interval when disruption stays under 10% of intervals for 3 sessions; shorten it by 30 s if it stays above; cut the break to 30 s once the interval reaches 240 s (Waller & Higbee, steps 7–8)."],[64.1,"O","A snack just before an instruction also works, with 10 s between trials so it never directly follows compliance (Ingvarsson et al., 2008)."],[64.1,"O","In these studies problem behavior still produced a short break, and NCR lowered it anyway; decide with the BCBA whether escape extinction is added, and add reinforcement for finished tasks if completion does not rise (Lomas Mevers et al., 2014)."],[64.1,"O","Mark every scheduled delivery as given or missed, and retrain staff at once after a lapse: at 80% integrity, fixed schedules lost the effect for most people (Jones et al., 2025)."],[64.7,"N","Lengthen the interval between breaks in small steps, and give no extra break after problem behavior."],[71.9,"N","Mark every scheduled delivery as given or missed, and retrain staff right away after a lapse. Missed deliveries can undo the effect."],[81.5,"N","Keep the pace of tasks the same as before. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "ncr_between": [[0.0,"O","Plenty of the item before the activity, then short, signaled periods without it."],[0.0,"O","Confirm that the item maintains the behavior (Rispoli et al., step 1)."],[0.0,"O","Define a rejection with the teacher, such as putting the item down and not picking it up within 3 s (step 2)."],[0.0,"O","During no-access periods keep another reinforcer available: attention when the behavior is maintained by items (Slocum et al., step 2)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Rispoli, M., O’Reilly, M., Lang, R., Machalicek, W., Davis, T., Lancioni, G., & Sigafoos, J. (2011). Effects of motivating operations on problem and academic behavior in classrooms. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 44(1), 187–192. Slocum, S. K., Grauerholz-Fisher, E., Peters, K. P., & Vollmer, T. R. (2018). A multicomponent approach to thinning reinforcer delivery during noncontingent reinforcement schedules. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 51(1), 61–69."],[0.6,"N","Noncontingent reinforcement before and between activities: a simulated example of the TD-1 cards after Rispoli and colleagues, 2011, and Slocum and colleagues, 2018."],[15.1,"N","Free access to the item just before work can lower its value during the work."],[20.7,"O","Just before the lesson: free access to the car."],[21.0,"N","Give the item freely until the student turns it down. Offer it again after each rejection. After the third, start the activity right away."],[31,"O","He pushes the car away; it is offered again, and he turns it down again (the study ended access after the third rejection)."],[34.8,"Teacher","Do you want the car?"],[39.0,"Teacher","Okay, let’s read."],[39.0,"O","The lesson starts right away, with the car in its usual place."],[41,"O","Later, practice short periods without the item, signaled by a card (Slocum et al., steps 5–7)."],[41.3,"N","Later, practice short periods without the item. Signal each one, keep attention available, and return the item when the card turns back."],[51.0,"Teacher","Three, two, one."],[51.0,"O","The card turns white and the car goes on the ledge; he keeps talking with the boy and opens the book."],[59.2,"Teacher","Here’s your car."],[60.2,"O","The card goes back to orange, and the car comes back right away."],[63.3,"O","Lengthen no-access time after each 2 sessions at or below 0.1 per min: 10 s, 30 s, 60 s, 120 s, then longer, with at least 1 min of access between periods; go back a level if it rises (Slocum et al., steps 8–9)."],[63.3,"O","For attention: 15 min of steady conversation just before a period when adults will be busy (O’Reilly et al., 2007)."],[63.3,"O","The study’s access periods were long (22 to 45 min); try a shorter one and check that it still works. Blocking attempts to take the item is restrictive: plan it with consent (Rispoli et al., step 8; Slocum et al., step 6)."],[63.3,"O","Mark every scheduled delivery as given or missed, and retrain staff at once after a lapse: at 80% integrity, fixed schedules lost the effect for most people (Jones et al., 2025)."],[63.9,"N","Lengthen the periods without the item only after two good sessions in a row."],[69.4,"N","Mark every scheduled delivery as given or missed, and retrain staff right away after a lapse. Missed deliveries can undo the effect."],[79.0,"N","For attention, fifteen minutes of steady conversation before a busy time works the same way. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "rird_vocal": [[0.0,"O","Interrupt vocal stereotypy and redirect to speech the student already has."],[0.0,"O","Confirm with a functional analysis that the vocal stereotypy is not socially maintained (Ahearn et al., step 1)."],[0.0,"O","List 5 or more questions the student already answers correctly and fluently, or vocal imitations (step 2)."],[0.0,"O","Answer appropriate speech at any time, with attention or the item asked for (step 3; Ahrens et al., step 7)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Ahearn, W. H., Clark, K. M., MacDonald, R. P. F., & Chung, B. I. (2007). Assessing and treating vocal stereotypy in children with autism. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 40(2), 263–275. Ahrens, E. N., Lerman, D. C., Kodak, T., Worsdell, A. S., & Keegan, C. (2011). Further evaluation of response interruption and redirection as treatment for stereotypy. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 44(1), 95–108."],[0.6,"N","Response interruption and redirection for vocal stereotypy: a simulated example of the TD-1 cards after Ahearn and colleagues, 2007, and Ahrens and colleagues, 2011."],[15.2,"N","Use it only when the stereotypy is not socially maintained. List questions the student already answers, and answer any appropriate talk right away."],[26,"O","He plays with the train."],[26.2,"N","When vocal stereotypy starts, say the student's name in a neutral tone and ask a known question. Keep going until three correct answers in a row, with no stereotypy. Then praise talking, and back to play."],[41.2,"O","He starts a repetitive humming sound. Right away, in a neutral tone."],[43.3,"Teacher","Leo."],[44.6,"Teacher","What’s your name?"],[44.6,"Student","Leo."],[46.5,"Teacher","What color is your shirt?"],[48.2,"Student","Yellow."],[48.2,"Teacher","How old are you?"],[49.9,"Student","Eight."],[50.8,"Teacher","Great job talking!"],[51.2,"O","Back to the train."],[53.6,"O","No answer within 5 s: repeat or model it (“Say ball”), then ask with the answer included, then move on. The sequence ends after 3 trials without stereotypy, answered or not (Ahrens et al., steps 3–6)."],[53.9,"N","If there is no answer, model it, or move on to the next question. The sequence ends after three trials without stereotypy, answered or not."],[64.5,"O","The one-question version (Toper-Korkmaz et al., 2018, steps 5–7)."],[64.8,"N","One question can be enough. In one study, three in a row added time and no benefit."],[74.5,"Teacher","Leo, what’s this?"],[76.4,"Student","Dog."],[77.9,"Teacher","Nice talking!"],[77.9,"O","The sequence ends at the first correct answer."],[80.2,"O","Pause the session clock during each sequence, and record how long the procedure takes (Ahearn et al., step 7; Toper-Korkmaz et al., step 9)."],[80.2,"O","Other cards in the bank redirect to echoes, comments about the toy, spelling, or the student’s least-liked mastered task."],[80.2,"O","Some studies also took the toy away during the sequence; that is a punishment step and needs approval before use (Colón & Ahearn, 2019, step 3)."],[80.2,"O","Train each adult with written steps, a video model and role play to 100%, observe at least twice a week, and retrain if it is used on fewer than half of the episodes (Colón & Ahearn, 2019, steps 8–10)."],[80.8,"N","Pause the session clock during each sequence, and record how much time the procedure takes."],[86.7,"N","Train every adult with practice and feedback, and check in short observations each week that it is used on every episode."],[95.0,"N","Interrupting is a restrictive step: plan it with consent. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "rird_motor": [[0.0,"O","Interrupt stereotypy and redirect to movements the student already does."],[0.0,"O","Confirm the stereotypy is automatically maintained, and that the student follows at least 10 one-step directions (Saini et al., step 1)."],[0.0,"O","Give two moderately preferred quiet toys, and reply briefly to appropriate talk (Cassella et al., steps 1 and 7)."],[0.0,"O","Use directions that need no speech: touch head, clap hands, stand up (step 2)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Cassella, M. D., Sidener, T. M., Sidener, D. W., & Progar, P. R. (2011). Response interruption and redirection for vocal stereotypy in children with autism: A systematic replication. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 44(1), 169–173. Saini, V., Gregory, M. K., Uran, K. J., & Fantetti, M. A. (2015). Parametric analysis of response interruption and redirection as treatment for stereotypy. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 48(1), 96–106."],[0.6,"N","Response interruption and redirection with motor directions: a simulated example of the TD-1 cards after Cassella and colleagues, 2011, and Saini and colleagues, 2015."],[15.1,"N","List one-step directions the student already follows, such as touch your head, or clap your hands."],[22.7,"O","She works on the puzzle."],[23.0,"N","At each instance of stereotypy, say the student's name and give a direction. Continue until three in a row are done without stereotypy, then give specific praise."],[35,"O","Her hands start flapping. Right away."],[35.1,"Teacher","Maya, touch your head."],[38.5,"Teacher","Clap your hands."],[40.8,"Teacher","Stand up."],[40.8,"O","Three in a row, with no flapping."],[42.1,"Teacher","Nice standing up!"],[43,"O","She sits back down to the puzzle."],[47.4,"O","If she does not respond within 5 s (Cassella et al., step 3)."],[47.7,"N","If she does not respond within five seconds, model the movement. Physical guidance is a restrictive last step."],[57.7,"Teacher","Maya, touch your nose."],[57.7,"O","No response, so a model, and she copies it."],[60.8,"Teacher","Nice touching your nose!"],[63.8,"O","One direction instead of three also worked, and took less time (Saini et al., 2015)."],[64.1,"N","One direction instead of three also worked, and takes less time."],[69,"O","Physical guidance after the model is restrictive: stop if the student pulls away, and plan it with consent (Saini et al., step 6)."],[69,"O","Some studies removed the toys during the sequence: a punishment step that needs approval before use (Saini et al., step 3)."],[69,"O","Train each adult with written steps, a video model and role play to 100%, observe at least twice a week, and retrain if it is used on fewer than half of the episodes (Colón & Ahearn, 2019, steps 8–10)."],[69.6,"N","Run it in at least two rooms with two adults, and probe now and then with someone new."],[75.3,"N","Train every adult with practice and feedback, and check in short observations each week that it is used on every episode."],[83.7,"N","Interrupting is a restrictive step: plan it with consent. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "rird_pica": [[0.0,"O","Stop the item before it reaches the mouth, then give the hand something better to do."],[0.0,"O","Sweep the area for small items before each session, and keep one adult watching at all times (McCord et al., step 2)."],[0.0,"O","Keep a preferred, nurse-approved food freely available (steps 3–4)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · McCord, B. E., Grosser, J. W., Iwata, B. A., & Powers, L. A. (2005). An analysis of response-blocking parameters in the prevention of pica. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 38(3), 391–394. Frank-Crawford, M. A., Hagopian, L. P., Scheithauer, M., McMahon, M. X. H., Argueta, T., Call, N. A., & Schmidt, J. D. (2025). Assessment and treatment of pica: A consecutive controlled case series study. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 58(4), 771–793."],[0.6,"N","Blocking and redirection for pica: a simulated example of the TD-1 cards after McCord and colleagues, 2005, and Frank-Crawford and colleagues, 2025."],[14.2,"N","Start with a written safety plan with the family, the doctor and the school nurse. Sweep the area before each session, and supervise closely."],[24.1,"O","Popcorn is freely available, and the adult stays close."],[24.4,"N","Block as soon as the student touches an inedible item, with a flat open hand between his hand and his mouth. Take the item away, and redirect him to the preferred food."],[35.8,"O","He picks up a scrap of paper and brings it toward his mouth."],[38.1,"O","At once: a flat open hand between his hand and his mouth, without holding him. A restrictive step: it needs consent."],[40.8,"Teacher","Have some popcorn."],[41.5,"O","The paper goes in the trash."],[45.8,"O","Teach him to throw items away instead, prompting at first (Frank-Crawford et al., step 4)."],[46.1,"N","Teach him to throw items away instead, and praise each one right away."],[51.4,"O","He drops the scrap in the trash."],[55.0,"Teacher","Nice throwing it away!"],[55.0,"O","And a high five, right away."],[59.9,"O","Block at the first touch every time, not when the item nears the mouth: late blocking did not reduce pica (McCord et al., step 6)."],[59.9,"O","Keep competing items that cut pica by at least 80% freely available, and move to new places only after pica is down by 90% (Frank-Crawford et al., steps 3 and 9)."],[59.9,"O","For motor stereotypy, the bank’s blocking cards use a brief touch, then hands held near for 20 s (DeRosa et al., 2019), or a redirect to a matched toy (Fisher et al., 1998c)."],[59.9,"O","Train every adult in role play to 90% first, and count pica, blocked attempts and discards in every session (Frank-Crawford et al., steps 8–9)."],[60.5,"N","Keep competing items freely available, and count attempts, blocks and discards in every session."],[67.4,"N","Train every adult with practice and feedback, and check in short observations each week that it is used on every episode."],[75.7,"N","Blocking is a restrictive step: plan it with consent. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "a_highp": [[0.0,"O","A strong reinforcer was picked: an edible or a top item, not praise alone (step 2)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Mace, F. C., Hock, M. L., Lalli, J. S., West, B. J., Belfiore, P., Pinter, E., & Brown, D. K. (1988). Behavioral momentum in the treatment of noncompliance. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 21(2), 123–141. Lemons, A., & Wilder, D. A. (2026). The effect of varied versus constant high-probability instructional sequences on cooperation. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 59(3), e70073."],[0.6,"N","High-probability request sequence, also called behavioral momentum, for escape-maintained behavior: a simulated example of a TD-1 bank card."],[12.9,"N","Before it starts, the team tests each request on ten probe trials. Easy requests are done on eighty percent or more; the hard one, on less than forty."],[24.8,"N","She gives three easy requests, one to three seconds apart. Each one he does earns praise and a snack."],[33.3,"Teacher","Give me a high five."],[37.3,"Teacher","Touch your nose."],[44.8,"Teacher","Show me a thumbs up."],[44.8,"O","Each is followed by praise and a snack in his palm."],[47.0,"Teacher","Nice thumbs up."],[50,"O","Right after the third, she gives the hard request."],[50.3,"Teacher","Now write your name on your math sheet."],[56.7,"Teacher","You got started. Great job."],[56.7,"O","And a snack."],[60.0,"N","Within three seconds of the third, she gives the hard request. When he starts it, the same praise and snack follow."],[68,"O","Next trial."],[69.0,"Teacher","Touch your nose."],[70.6,"N","In the next trial, he does not touch his nose within ten seconds. No snack, and no hard request."],[79.1,"N","She waits thirty seconds, then starts over with three new easy requests."],[84.7,"O","A different order from the last trial: thumbs up, then high five, then nose."],[85.0,"N","The new set comes in a different order. Only after all three does the hard request come."],[92.9,"Teacher","Show me a thumbs up."],[95.4,"Teacher","Nice thumbs up."],[98.2,"Teacher","Give me a high five."],[98.2,"O","Each one done earns praise and a snack."],[103.7,"Teacher","Touch your nose."],[103.7,"O","Only after all three are done does the hard request come."],[109.4,"O","Right after the third, the hard request."],[109.6,"Teacher","Now write your name on your math sheet."],[113.9,"O","He starts, and the same praise and snack follow."],[116.2,"Teacher","You got started. Great job."],[124.7,"O","Weeks later, as he keeps cooperating: fewer easy requests before the hard one."],[125.0,"N","As he keeps cooperating, the easy requests are faded: here, one easy request, then the hard one."],[132.7,"Teacher","Give me a high five."],[132.8,"O","One easy request."],[132.8,"O","With praise and a snack, then the hard request, then praise and a snack again."],[134.4,"Teacher","Nice."],[139.8,"Teacher","Now write your name on your math sheet."],[143.2,"Teacher","You got started. Great job."],[148.9,"O","For escape-maintained behavior, easy requests alone do not override escape: reinforce the hard request first; escape extinction is a separate, marked step, with consent if guidance is used."],[149.5,"N","Before you start: test each request first, and pick a strong reinforcer."],[155.7,"N","If he does not do the hard request, follow the Responding to behavior sheet for his function."],[162.0,"N","This is a simulated example. Adapt every step to the learner's own plan."]],
 "a_highp_praise": [[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Wilder, D. A., Sheppard, C., Izquierdo, F., & Flynn, K. (2025). A comparison of medium probability versus high probability instructions to increase cooperation in the context of the high probability instructional sequence. Behavioral Interventions, 40(1), e70001."],[0.6,"N","High-probability request sequence with praise only: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Wilder, Sheppard and colleagues, 2025."],[12.9,"N","The parent and teacher nominate instructions, and each is tried twenty times. Easy ones are followed eighty percent of the time or more; the hard one, thirty percent or less."],[25.2,"N","She gives three easy requests, only one to two seconds apart, with a brief word of praise for each one. Praise only: no snack."],[35.1,"Teacher","Great."],[35.2,"Teacher","Show me a thumbs up."],[37.2,"Teacher","Nice."],[38.1,"Teacher","Touch your nose."],[39.2,"Teacher","Good."],[39.9,"Teacher","Give me a high five."],[41.3,"O","One to two seconds after the third."],[41.3,"Teacher","Now write your name on your math sheet."],[46.2,"Teacher","Good."],[48.4,"N","One to two seconds after the third, the hard request. He starts it, and the same brief praise follows."],[55.9,"O","Another trial, after three easy requests in a new order."],[56.2,"N","In another trial he does not start the hard request. She says nothing and does nothing."],[62.9,"Teacher","Now write your name on your math sheet."],[62.9,"O","He does not. No repeat, no prompt, no reaction."],[70.0,"N","If he misses an easy request, she stops the sequence. No praise, and no hard request."],[76.8,"Teacher","Touch your nose."],[76.8,"O","Not done within 2 seconds. She stops: no praise, no hard request."],[85.1,"N","She waits fifteen seconds, then starts again from the first easy request."],[90.9,"N","Run three trials a session, and record every instruction, restarts included."],[96.7,"N","If the hard request is not done, follow the Responding to behavior sheet for the function. Adapt every step to the learner’s plan."]],
 "a_highp_class": [[0.0,"O","Write the transition as single steps in a set order, with 20 s for each (step 2)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Ardoin, S. P., Martens, B. K., & Wolfe, L. A. (1999). Using high-probability instruction sequences with fading to increase student compliance during transitions. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 32(3), 339–351."],[0.6,"N","High-probability instructions for the whole class, before a transition: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Ardoin and colleagues, 1999."],[13.5,"N","First, keep only the short actions that every student follows every time."],[19,"O","Three short actions every student already follows, given to the whole class."],[19.3,"N","The teacher gives the class three easy instructions, no more than five seconds apart."],[25.2,"Teacher","Everybody, hands on your head."],[27.3,"Teacher","Hands in your lap."],[29.1,"Teacher","Eyes on me."],[29.1,"O","About two seconds apart."],[31.1,"N","Within five seconds of the last one comes the transition instruction."],[35.6,"Teacher","Now close your math books and put them under your desks."],[39.1,"N","She praises the named target students who followed it, and the others too."],[44.1,"Teacher","Thank you, Sam. Thank you, Maya. Nice job, everyone."],[49.4,"N","As the target students keep following the transitions, the easy instructions are faded: here, just one, with the same five-second gap."],[59.3,"Teacher","Everybody, stand up."],[62.2,"Teacher","Now line up at the door."],[66.1,"Teacher","Thank you, Sam. Nice lining up, everyone."],[69.7,"O","Record for each target student whether each transition was followed within 20 s (step 9)."],[70.3,"N","Now and then, give the transition with no easy instructions first, to see whether following holds on its own."],[77.9,"N","Rotate a wide set of easy instructions so they stay fresh. Adapt every step to the class and the plan."]],
 "a_highp_seatwork": [[0.0,"O","Three short math requests, then “start on your own”."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Wehby, J. H., & Hollahan, M. S. (2000). Effects of high-probability requests on the latency to initiate academic tasks. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 33(2), 259–262."],[0.6,"N","High-probability requests before independent seatwork: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Wehby and Hollahan, 2000."],[11.8,"N","The list holds short requests tied to the assignment, each one followed right away at least eighty-five percent of the time."],[20.1,"N","After the board examples, the teacher stands beside her desk and gives three requests from the list, each within ten seconds of the last, with praise for each."],[30.3,"Teacher","Great."],[30.6,"Teacher","Write your name at the top."],[34.2,"Teacher","Good."],[34.8,"Teacher","Circle number one."],[37.4,"Teacher","Nice."],[37.4,"Teacher","Underline the directions."],[40.5,"N","Within ten seconds of the third, he asks her to begin the seatwork. She starts, so he praises her and walks away."],[49.3,"Teacher","Now start the problems on your own."],[51.1,"Teacher","Good start."],[51.1,"O","And he walks away."],[54.6,"N","If she does not follow a request within ten seconds, he gives another from the list, until three in a row are followed."],[63.0,"Teacher","Circle number one."],[63.0,"O","Not followed within 10 seconds, so another from the list."],[66.9,"Teacher","Write the date next to your name."],[71.1,"Teacher","Good."],[73.6,"N","If the work does not start within ten seconds, walk away without comment."],[79.3,"N","Record the seconds until work starts, and whether she is working during the first ten minutes. Adapt every step to the student’s plan."]],
 "a_highp_peer": [[0.0,"O","Easy play requests are ones he does 80% of the time or more; the social requests, under 50% though he is able to (step 1)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Davis, C. A., & Reichle, J. (1996). Variant and invariant high-probability requests: Increasing appropriate behaviors in children with emotional-behavioral disorders. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 29(4), 471–482."],[0.6,"N","High-probability requests given by a peer: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Davis and Reichle, 1996."],[11.8,"N","Three classmates learn the sequence first: it is described, shown, role-played, then practiced with the teacher’s cues."],[21.1,"N","The teacher cues the peer quietly, from behind the student. The peer gives three easy play requests, with a play comment after each one."],[30.9,"Peer","Put the cup on the table."],[34.1,"Peer","Cool!"],[35.0,"Peer","Hold the pizza."],[36.4,"Peer","Yum, pizza!"],[38.2,"Peer","Put it on a plate."],[39.8,"Peer","Nice."],[41.3,"N","Within five seconds of the third, the social request. He does it, and his classmate answers, so the play goes on."],[49.3,"Peer","Give the pizza to Ben."],[49.3,"Classmate","Thanks for the pizza! Can I have the banana?"],[50.8,"O","Ben answers, and the play goes on."],[54.6,"O","Another time, a request is not done."],[54.9,"N","If he does not do a request, the peer waits about thirty seconds, then starts a new sequence."],[62.1,"Peer","Hold the banana."],[62.1,"O","He does not. The peer does not repeat it and keeps playing; about 30 seconds later, a new sequence."],[69.5,"O","Cue the peer quietly, by a whisper away from the student or a hand signal from behind, and fade the cues as the peer can run it alone (step 10)."],[70.2,"N","Fade the teacher’s cues as the peer can run the sequence alone."],[75.1,"N","Plan about eight social requests in a ten-minute play session. Adapt every step to the student’s plan."]],
 "fct-practical-guide-thinning": [[0.0,"O","The card's ten steps, from the first prompt to a new place."],[0.0,"O","The team picked a low-effort request: a BREAK card (step 2)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Tiger, J. H., Hanley, G. P., & Bruzek, J. (2008). Functional communication training: A review and practical guide. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 1(1), 16–23."],[0.8,"N","Functional communication training for escape-maintained behavior: a simulated example of a TD-1 bank card. Before teaching starts, a functional analysis confirms escape, and the team picks a break card as the request."],[18.5,"N","The instructor presents the task: the situation where problem behavior used to start."],[25.2,"N","Before he gets upset, she points to the break card and waits. Over trials, the prompt fades."],[32.9,"Instructor","Sure, take a break."],[37.1,"N","He hands her the card, and she honors it right away."],[43.4,"N","Later, he pushes the work away and turns away."],[48.0,"N","Problem behavior does not earn the break. The work stays, and she points to the card."],[54.6,"N","When he asks with the card, the break comes right away."],[59.2,"Instructor","Thanks for asking. Take a break."],[62.5,"N","Weeks later, the instructor thins the schedule: \"Two more problems first, then your break.\""],[69.6,"N","During the wait, she sets a small fidget beside him, and he keeps working."],[75.0,"Instructor","Great work, take your break."],[76.8,"N","After the work, the break comes. When the timer runs out, he comes back."],[82.4,"Instructor","Welcome back."],[84.1,"N","A new adult in a new place: a supply-room job with another staff member."],[89.9,"O","The request has grown. He gives the card and asks."],[90.5,"Student","Can I have a break, please?"],[90.5,"O","The break is honored."],[92.4,"Instructor","Sure, take a break."],[93.4,"N","The request has grown to a full sentence, and the new staff member honors it."],[99.4,"O","Confirm the function with a functional analysis."],[99.7,"N","Confirm the function first. Before using escape extinction, complete the consent block on TD-1's Responding to behavior sheet. This is a simulated example: adapt every step to the learner's own plan."]],
 "fct_attention": [[0.0,"O","Confirm their function with 5-minute tests that put consequences on the warning behavior only (step 2)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Najdowski, A. C., Wallace, M. D., Ellsworth, C. L., MacAleese, A. N., & Cleveland, J. M. (2008). Functional analyses and treatment of precursor behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 41(1), 97–105."],[0.6,"N","Functional communication training for attention: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Najdowski and colleagues, 2008."],[11.6,"N","First, ask staff which behaviors come just before the serious ones, and confirm with brief tests that these warning signs get attention. At the start of each session, give the student a card to raise when he wants the teacher."],[26.9,"O","Seatwork. His request card (yellow) is on his desk; she is at her table."],[27.2,"N","His team's warning signs are stopping work, crossing his arms and slumping. When they happen, there is no reprimand and no redirection. The teacher says one thing only."],[39.9,"O","A warning sign the team listed: he stops working and crosses his arms."],[46.0,"Teacher","Remember to use your card if you need something."],[46.0,"O","That is all: no reprimand, no redirection."],[52.2,"O","A little later."],[52.5,"N","When he raises the card instead, she answers right away."],[56.7,"O","He raises his card."],[61.8,"O","At once."],[62.7,"Teacher","Good, what do you need?"],[70.7,"N","She gives five to ten seconds of attention, then goes back to her work. If a student needs more support, prompt the card on a short timer, and lengthen it after three prompted requests in a row with no warning signs."],[84.4,"O","For a student who needs more support, prompt the card at short fixed intervals and lengthen the interval after 3 prompted requests in a row with no warning behavior (step 6)."],[85.2,"N","Once requests are steady, lengthen sessions from ten to thirty minutes, and move into the natural setting. Compare daily counts of serious behavior before and after."],[96.5,"N","This is one boy in one classroom, and the study did not thin his requests, so plan that next, with a signaled schedule. The study's warning signs were whining and complaining; this video shows silent ones. Use the warning signs found for your student."],[114.2,"N","Warning signs are not the dangerous behavior. If severe behavior happens, follow the student's crisis plan. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "fct_tangible": [[0.0,"O","A picture card earns favorite things at once; later the same request meets a short wait, and sometimes a no."],[0.0,"O","Run about 5 trials a day, alternating teaching trials with free-access trials (step 7)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Fahmie, T. A., Iwata, B. A., & Mead, S. C. (2016). Within-subject analysis of a prevention strategy for problem behavior. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 49(4), 915–926."],[0.6,"N","Functional communication training for items, with waiting and accepting no: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Fahmie and colleagues, 2016."],[13.2,"N","With the teachers, list the item situations that set off minor protest, such as favorite things in sight but out of reach, and test each one briefly. The request card stays within the student's reach at all times."],[28.6,"N","Each two-minute trial starts by setting up one of those situations. Here, his music things are on the teacher's side of the table, where he can see them but not reach them."],[39.6,"O","He hands over his card."],[46.3,"Teacher","Here you go."],[53.5,"N","If he does not hand over the card, a second adult points to it, and about two seconds later guides his hand, stopping if he pulls away. Free-access trials, with the items and attention, are mixed in."],[67.7,"N","Once his requests are independent, some trials have a wait. The teacher says wait and shows the red card for thirty seconds. Then the items come."],[79.2,"Teacher","Wait."],[79.2,"O","The red card goes up: 30 seconds of waiting (shortened here)."],[84.8,"O","Time up."],[85.0,"Teacher","Thanks for waiting."],[85.0,"O","Then the items."],[92.4,"N","In the last phase, some requests get a no. The red card stays up for the rest of the trial, and the items do not come."],[101.6,"Teacher","No."],[101.6,"O","The red card stays up for the rest of the trial, with no items. He stays calm."],[108.8,"O","Lengthen the wait before the prompt by about 2 s after 3 trials in a row without an independent request (step 6)."],[109.8,"N","Move to each new phase after fifteen training trials in a row with one prompt or fewer, and no requests in fifteen free-access trials. Record the most severe behavior in every trial."],[123.0,"N","This is one young man in a school therapy room, with short follow-up, so the prevention effect is preliminary. In an untrained work situation his behavior grew, so plan help or break requests for work too."],[137.5,"N","Guiding his hand to the card is an ordinary prompt that stops if he pulls away. Never reinforce severe behavior to test it, as the study's test trials did. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "fct_thinning_mult": [[0.0,"O","A signal shows when the taught request will be honored, and the not-now time grows step by step."],[0.0,"O","Confirm the function with a functional analysis and record baseline in 5- or 10-minute sessions (step 1)."],[0.0,"O","Fade the prompt by waiting 0, 2, 5, 10 and, if needed, 20 s, at least 2 sessions at each (step 3)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Greer, B. D., Fisher, W. W., Saini, V., Owen, T. M., & Jones, J. K. (2016). Functional communication training during reinforcement schedule thinning: An analysis of 25 applications. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 49(1), 105–121."],[0.6,"N","Schedule thinning after functional communication training, with signals for when to ask: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Greer and colleagues, 2016."],[13.6,"N","First, confirm the function and teach the request. Set up the situation, guide the student to the card at once, then fade the prompt from zero to ten or twenty seconds. Honor every request, and give nothing for problem behavior."],[30.2,"N","Her problem behavior gets the teacher's attention, so her card shows the two of them together. Once problem behavior is low, the signal is added. On green, every request is honored."],[42.9,"O","She touches her card. At once."],[44.2,"Teacher","Hi! Tell me about your picture."],[44.2,"O","20–30 seconds of attention (shortened here)."],[51.6,"N","On red, requests are not honored. The teacher is busy and does not answer or look. Red starts at about two seconds."],[60.8,"O","Red: a request now gets no answer, and no look. She goes back to coloring."],[67.3,"O","Red time is up: back to green. Her next request will be honored."],[74.3,"O","Step up: red longer, green shorter."],[74.8,"N","Lengthen red toward four minutes only when problem behavior is at least ninety percent below baseline, and at least seventy percent of requests come on green."],[84.8,"O","Lengthen red only when problem behavior is at least 90% below baseline and at least 70% of requests come on green (step 6); most applications ended at 60 s green and 240 s red."],[84.8,"O","A record review of clinic cases, with no school data: in class, run green and red inside real routines, and later move to natural busy and free cues (Kuhn et al., 2010)."],[85.5,"N","If she keeps asking on red, take the card away during red instead. If problem behavior stays high, add a preferred item during red. The study needed that in six of twenty-five cases."],[99.2,"N","This was a review of clinic cases, not a controlled comparison, and it has no data from school. Most ended at one minute of green and four minutes of red."],[109.9,"N","For escape, withholding the break after problem behavior is escape extinction, so complete the consent block first. Time-out and blocking are restrictive steps that need review and consent. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "choice-academic-task-escape": [[0.0,"O","Before work, the student picks which of his usual assignments to do, and may switch at any time."],[0.0,"O","Before each 5-minute session, set out 4 to 6 of them (step 2)."],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Romaniuk, C., Miltenberger, R., Conyers, C., Jenner, N., Jurgens, M., & Ringenberg, C. (2002). The influence of activity choice on problem behaviors maintained by escape versus attention. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 35(4), 349–362."],[0.6,"N","Offering a choice of task, for escape-maintained problem behavior: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Romaniuk and colleagues, 2002."],[13.3,"N","First, confirm with brief test sessions that problem behavior is maintained by escape from tasks. In the study, choice helped escape-maintained behavior, but not attention-maintained behavior. Then ask the teacher to list eight to ten tasks that usually set off problem behavior."],[33.7,"N","Before each five-minute work session, set out the materials for four to six of those tasks; two are shown here. Ask which assignment the student would like to work on, start the one he picks, and tell him he can switch at any time."],[49.4,"O","Two of his usual tasks are set out: math and spelling."],[51.3,"O","She asks which one he would like; he points to math."],[51.4,"Teacher","Which assignment would you like to work on today?"],[56.7,"O","She starts the one he picked and tells him he can switch at any time."],[56.8,"Teacher","Math first. You can switch any time; just ask."],[64.2,"O","Later in the session, he is working on math."],[64.5,"N","Honor every request to switch. If he does not start, give the instruction; after five more seconds with no start, show him how."],[74.5,"O","He asks to switch to spelling."],[77.3,"O","The switch is honored right away."],[77.5,"Teacher","Sure, you can switch."],[80.3,"O","He does not start."],[83.1,"O","The instruction."],[83.2,"Teacher","Write your first word."],[84.4,"O","He starts. (After 5 s with no start, she would show him how.)"],[89.7,"O","Check the function first: choice helped escape-maintained behavior, not attention-maintained behavior (praise for working and ignoring problem behavior helped those students)."],[89.7,"O","Set out 4 to 6 tasks from the teacher’s 8 to 10 (two are shown here), honor every switch request, and keep offering choices: problem behavior rose each time choice was removed."],[90.3,"N","Respond to problem behavior as the student's plan states. In the study, each problem behavior still earned a ten-second break, so the drop came from choice alone. Keep offering choices once he has started: when choice was taken away, problem behavior rose. Record the percentage of session time with problem behavior."],[112.4,"N","In the study, problem behavior fell from about seventy percent of session time to between two and twenty-seven percent for the students whose behavior was maintained by escape. The sessions were five minutes long, with a therapist in a separate room. A physical prompt to start stops if the student pulls away; guiding a refused task is escape extinction and needs consent first. Adapt every step to the student's plan."]],
 "gbg": [[0.0,"O","Each rule break earns a mark for the student’s team; teams with the fewest marks, or 5 or fewer, win privileges"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Barrish, H. H., Saunders, M., & Wolf, M. M. (1969). Good behavior game: Effects of individual contingencies for group consequences on disruptive behavior in a classroom. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 2(2), 119–124."],[0.6,"N","Good Behavior Game: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Barrish and colleagues, 1969."],[9.2,"O","Game rules: 1. Stay seated. 2. Raise a hand and wait. 3. No talking out."],[9.4,"N","First, define the rules and teach them with examples: stay seated unless given permission, raise a hand and wait to be called on, and no talking to neighbors or calling out."],[23.0,"Teacher","Stay in your seat. Raise your hand and wait."],[27.2,"O","Team 1 and Team 2: the two rows."],[27.4,"N","Split the class into two teams, for example by rows. Play every day, in one subject period."],[35.8,"Teacher","This row is Team One. This row is Team Two."],[41.9,"O","Marks this period: Team 1, 0; Team 2, 0. Wins with 5 or fewer."],[42.1,"N","Each time a student breaks a rule, put a mark on the board for that team, without comment."],[49.6,"O","Out of seat, no permission."],[51.7,"O","Team 2 mark, no comment. Team 1, 0; Team 2, 1."],[56.3,"O","Marks this period: Team 1, 2; Team 2, 3. Wins with 5 or fewer."],[56.6,"N","At the end of the period, every team with the fewest marks, or five or fewer, wins. On the first day, allow ten or fewer."],[66.6,"Teacher","Both teams have five marks or fewer. Both teams win!"],[69.2,"O","Both teams win."],[72.0,"N","Winners get privileges: winner tags, stars on the winners chart, lining up first for lunch, and special-project time."],[80.8,"O","Winner tags for both teams."],[89.3,"O","A weekly bonus: an extra privilege, such as 4 minutes of early recess, for any team with 20 marks or fewer that week (step 7)."],[89.3,"O","Once behavior improves, add a second period and count both periods as one game (step 8)."],[89.5,"N","A team with twenty marks or fewer in a week earns an extra privilege, such as early recess. Once behavior improves, add a second period, and count both as one game."],[102.0,"O","If one student keeps earning most of a team’s marks, or refuses to play, take that student out of team scoring and use an individual plan (step 9)."],[102.0,"O","Watch for peer pressure on students who earn many marks."],[102.3,"N","If one student keeps earning most of a team's marks, or refuses to play, use an individual plan instead. Watch for peer pressure."],[112.3,"O","In the study, the losing team kept working through the free-time period (step 6). That is punishment: it needs approval and written consent before use, and it is not shown here."],[112.6,"N","In the study, losing teams kept working during free time. That is punishment: it needs approval and written consent, and it is not shown here."],[123.3,"O","Data: the percentage of 1-minute intervals in which any student was out of seat or talking out, and each team’s marks."],[123.3,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[123.6,"N","Record the percent of one-minute intervals in which any student was out of seat or talking out, and the team marks. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "chain_backward": [[0.0,"O","Guide the early steps and teach the last step first; then guide one fewer step at a time"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Slocum, S. K., & Tiger, J. H. (2011). An assessment of the efficiency of and child preference for forward and backward chaining. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 44(4), 793–805."],[0.6,"N","Backward chaining: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Slocum and Tiger, 2011."],[8.7,"O","The chain: 1. Pat head. 2. Pat lap. 3. Clap. Baseline: no help, no feedback."],[9.0,"N","First, write the task analysis. Here the chain has three steps: pat your head, pat your lap, then clap. Run at least three baseline trials: give the instruction, with no help and no feedback."],[25.2,"O","He picks an item to earn."],[25.5,"N","Before each session, let the student pick one item to earn."],[31.6,"O","He picks the car."],[35.4,"O","The chain: 1. Pat head, guided. 2. Pat lap, guided. 3. Clap, alone. Target: the last step."],[35.7,"N","Give the instruction, and right away guide the student through every step before the target. At first, that is every step but the last. Stop if the student pulls away."],[47.8,"Teacher","Do the star dance."],[57.5,"O","Waiting up to 5 s."],[57.8,"N","Then wait five seconds for the student to do the last step alone."],[67.8,"O","Done alone: praise, 30 s with the car."],[68.1,"N","Praise, and give thirty seconds with the chosen item, only when the target steps are done alone and in order."],[75.8,"Teacher","You did it! Take your car."],[82.7,"O","1. Pat head, guided. 2. Pat lap, alone. 3. Clap, alone. After 3 alone in a row: one fewer guided."],[83.0,"N","After three independent trials in a row, guide one fewer step. Now the student does the last two steps alone."],[97.4,"O","No response within 5 s, or an error: model the steps and give another chance (step 6)."],[97.4,"O","Still no response: physically guide the target steps, stopping if the student pulls away (step 7)."],[97.7,"N","If the student does not respond within five seconds, or makes an error, model the steps and give another chance. If there is still no response, guide the target steps, stopping if the student pulls away."],[111.8,"O","Run 10-trial sessions until the whole chain is done alone on 3 trials in a row (step 9)."],[111.8,"O","Data: each trial coded independent, correct after a model, or physically guided."],[112.1,"N","Run ten-trial sessions until the whole chain is done alone on three trials in a row. Record each trial as independent, correct after a model, or physically guided."],[124.8,"O","In the study, forward and backward chaining worked about equally well, and the students liked both. Choose by the task."],[124.8,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[125.1,"N","In the study, forward and backward chaining worked about equally well, and the students liked both. Choose by the task. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "chain_forward": [[0.0,"O","Teach the first step alone and guide the rest; then add one step at a time, in order"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Slocum, S. K., & Tiger, J. H. (2011). An assessment of the efficiency of and child preference for forward and backward chaining. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 44(4), 793–805."],[0.6,"N","Forward chaining: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Slocum and Tiger, 2011."],[8.7,"O","The chain: 1. Pat head. 2. Pat lap. 3. Clap. Baseline: no help, no feedback."],[9.0,"N","First, write the task analysis. Here the chain has three steps: pat your head, pat your lap, then clap. Run at least three baseline trials: give the instruction, with no help and no feedback."],[25.2,"O","He picks an item to earn."],[25.5,"N","Before each session, let the student pick one item to earn."],[31.6,"O","He picks the car."],[35.4,"O","The chain: 1. Pat head, alone. 2. Pat lap, guided. 3. Clap, guided. Target: the first step. Wait up to 5 s."],[35.7,"N","Give the instruction, and wait five seconds for the student to do the target steps alone. At first, that is only the first step."],[44.9,"Teacher","Do the star dance."],[51.2,"O","1. Pat head, alone. 2. Pat lap, guided. 3. Clap, guided. Done alone: praise, then guide the rest."],[51.5,"N","When it is done correctly, praise, then guide the student through the steps not yet taught. Stop if the student pulls away."],[71.6,"O","1. Pat head, alone. 2. Pat lap, guided. 3. Clap, guided. End of the chain: 30 s with the car."],[71.9,"N","At the end of the chain, give thirty seconds with the chosen item."],[76.6,"Teacher","You did it! Take your car."],[83.4,"O","1. Pat head, alone. 2. Pat lap, alone. 3. Clap, guided. After 3 alone in a row: add the next step."],[83.7,"N","After three independent trials in a row, add the next step. Now the student does the first two steps alone, in order, and you guide only the last."],[95.0,"Teacher","Do the star dance."],[108.2,"O","1. Pat head, alone. 2. Pat lap, alone. 3. Clap, alone. The whole chain alone."],[108.5,"N","Keep adding one step at a time, until the student does the whole chain alone."],[114.2,"Teacher","Do the star dance."],[120.2,"Teacher","You did it! Take your car."],[127.1,"O","No start within 5 s, an error, or a pause over 1 s between mastered steps: model the target steps and give another chance (step 6)."],[127.1,"O","Still not done: physically guide the target steps and then the rest, stopping if the student pulls away (step 7)."],[127.4,"N","If the student does not start within five seconds, makes an error, or pauses more than one second between mastered steps, model the target steps and give another chance. If it is still not done, guide the target steps and then the rest, stopping if the student pulls away."],[145.7,"O","Run 10-trial sessions until the whole chain is done alone on 3 trials in a row (step 9)."],[145.7,"O","Data: each trial coded independent, correct after a model, or physically guided."],[146.0,"N","Run ten-trial sessions until the whole chain is done alone on three trials in a row. Record each trial as independent, correct after a model, or physically guided."],[158.7,"O","In the study, forward and backward chaining worked about equally well, and the students liked both. Choose by the task."],[158.7,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[159.0,"N","In the study, forward and backward chaining worked about equally well, and the students liked both. Choose by the task. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "chain_hw_forward": [[0.0,"O","One training step at a time, from the start of the chain; the adult guides every step after it"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Preas, E. J., & Mathews, T. L. (2021). Evaluation of caregiver training procedures to teach activities of daily living skills. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 14(4), 958–973."],[0.6,"N","Forward chaining with handwashing: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Preas and Mathews, 2021."],[9.8,"O","The chain: 1. Water on, mastered. 2. Wet hands, training. 3. Soap, guided. 4. Scrub, guided. 5. Rinse, guided. 6. Water off and dry, guided. Probes with no prompts, then mark each step."],[10.1,"N","First, run at least three probes with no prompts, and mark each step of the task analysis: mastered, the one training step, or not yet taught. In this example the chain has six steps. Turning on the water is mastered. Wetting his hands is the training step."],[30.2,"O","Get attention; give the instruction."],[30.5,"N","Get the student's attention, then give the instruction."],[35.1,"Teacher","Wash your hands."],[38.1,"O","Now: step 1, water on. Mastered: wait 2 s, then a gesture, then physical."],[38.4,"N","On a mastered step, wait two seconds. If he errs or does not respond, give a gesture prompt first, then a physical prompt if needed."],[55.1,"O","Now: step 2, wet hands. Training step: wait 2 s."],[55.4,"N","On the training step, wait two seconds too."],[64.1,"O","Now: step 2, wet hands. Done alone: specific praise within 2 s."],[64.4,"N","He does it alone, so give specific praise within two seconds."],[69.2,"O","The rest: hand over hand, no reinforcer."],[69.5,"N","Then guide him, hand over hand, through every remaining step, in order, with no reinforcer for those steps."],[77.7,"O","Now: step 3, soap. The rest: hand over hand, no reinforcer."],[81.4,"N","Soap."],[86.6,"O","Now: step 4, scrub. The rest: hand over hand, no reinforcer."],[87.2,"N","Scrub."],[92.4,"O","Now: step 5, rinse. The rest: hand over hand, no reinforcer."],[93.0,"N","Rinse."],[98.2,"O","Now: step 6, water off and dry. The rest: hand over hand, no reinforcer."],[98.8,"N","Water off."],[104.6,"N","And dry."],[108.1,"O","Training step alone: praise and the item."],[108.4,"N","At the end of the chain, give specific praise for the training step, and a preferred item, only if the training step was done alone."],[117.9,"Teacher","You got your hands wet by yourself! Great job!"],[122.2,"O","Now: step 2, wet hands. Prompted: no praise, and no item at the end."],[122.5,"N","On another trial, he does not start the training step within two seconds. Prompt it hand over hand, with no praise. Guide the rest as before, and at the end, hold back both the praise and the item."],[141.9,"O","The chain: 1. Water on, mastered. 2. Wet hands, mastered. 3. Soap, training. 4. Scrub, guided. 5. Rinse, guided. 6. Water off and dry, guided. Now: step 3, soap. Alone on 2 trials in a row: the next step."],[142.2,"N","When the training step is done alone on two trials in a row, it is mastered, and its prompt fades to a gesture. The next step, getting soap, becomes the training step."],[160.5,"O","Run about 5 trials in a session, and teach more often than the study's 1 to 2 sessions a week (step 8)."],[160.5,"O","Data: each step independent or prompted, on Form SA-1."],[160.8,"N","Run about five trials in a session, and teach more often than the study's one or two sessions a week. Record each step as independent or prompted."],[171.4,"O","A parent or paraprofessional running the routine: coach during live trials until 90% correct across 2 sessions in a row (step 9)."],[171.4,"O","Guiding the untaught steps is physical guidance. If the student resists it, complete TD-1's consent block first."],[171.7,"N","If a parent or a paraprofessional will run the routine, coach them during live trials until they reach ninety percent correct across two sessions in a row. If the student resists hand-over-hand guidance, complete the consent block on Form TD-1 first."],[188.9,"O","In the study, parents learned the teaching steps quickly, but with 1 to 2 sessions a week the children gained few steps. Plan daily practice."],[188.9,"O","This is a simulated example with a six-step task analysis. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[189.2,"N","In the study, parents learned the teaching steps quickly, but with only one or two sessions a week the children gained few steps. So plan daily practice. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "chain_hw_backward": [[0.0,"O","Guide the earlier steps; teach the last step first, with only as much help as the student needs"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Realon, R. E., Wheeler, A. J., Spring, B., & Springer, M. (1986). Evaluating the quality of training delivered by direct-care staff in a state mental retardation center. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 1(3), 199–212."],[0.6,"N","Backward chaining with handwashing: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Realon and colleagues, 1986."],[10.3,"O","The chain: 1. Water on, guided. 2. Wet hands, guided. 3. Soap, guided. 4. Scrub, guided. 5. Rinse, guided. 6. Water off and dry, training. The last step is taught first."],[10.6,"N","Have the materials ready, and give the command within thirty seconds of the student arriving, exactly as it is written on the data sheet."],[19.6,"Teacher","Wash your hands."],[22.6,"O","Earlier steps: full guidance, no talking."],[22.9,"N","The last step of the chain is taught first. So fully guide him through every step that comes before it. After the command, do not talk to him, apart from the reinforcement."],[34.5,"O","Now: step 1, water on. Earlier steps: full guidance, no talking."],[38.2,"N","Water on."],[43.4,"O","Now: step 2, wet hands. Earlier steps: full guidance, no talking."],[44.0,"N","Wet hands."],[47.8,"O","Now: step 3, soap. Earlier steps: full guidance, no talking."],[48.4,"N","Soap."],[53.5,"O","Now: step 4, scrub. Earlier steps: full guidance, no talking."],[54.1,"N","Scrub."],[59.3,"O","Now: step 5, rinse. Earlier steps: full guidance, no talking."],[59.9,"N","Rinse."],[65.1,"O","Cue sequence: 1. Wait: alone? 2. Gesture: point or model. 3. Light physical prompt. 4. Full guidance. Now: 1. Wait: alone?"],[65.4,"N","At the training step, here turning off the water and drying his hands, step back and wait for him to do it alone."],[80.2,"O","Now: 2. Gesture: point or model."],[80.5,"N","He turns off the water, then stops. So follow the cue sequence: first a gesture, a point or a model; then a light physical prompt; then full guidance. Give only as much help as he needs."],[96.3,"O","Now: 2. Gesture: point or model. Here a point at the towel is enough."],[97.7,"N","Here, a point at the towel is enough."],[107.3,"O","Now: step 6, water off and dry. Reinforce at once, in at least 3 forms."],[107.6,"N","Reinforce right away, and with enthusiasm, using at least three forms: praise, touch, looking at him, telling him what he did well, or an edible if the team approves."],[121.7,"Teacher","You dried your hands! Great job!"],[126.0,"O","The chain: 1. Water on, guided. 2. Wet hands, guided. 3. Soap, guided. 4. Scrub, guided. 5. Rinse, guided. 6. Water off and dry, gesture. Now: step 6, water off and dry. Data sheet: step 6, gesture."],[126.3,"N","Then fill in the data sheet: the step trained, and the level of help he needed."],[132.4,"O","The chain: 1. Water on, guided. 2. Wet hands, guided. 3. Soap, guided. 4. Scrub, guided. 5. Rinse, training. 6. Water off and dry, alone. Then teach the step before it."],[132.7,"N","When he does the last step alone, the step before it becomes the training step. Now you guide the first four steps, and he rinses, turns off the water, and dries his hands."],[144.4,"O","Now: step 4, scrub. Then teach the step before it."],[147.6,"O","Now: step 5, rinse. Then teach the step before it."],[153.7,"O","Now: step 6, water off and dry. Then teach the step before it."],[165.3,"O","Have a supervisor observe a session each week with the 7-item checklist, and give the staff member feedback and praise (step 8)."],[165.3,"O","Data: the training step and the level of help each session, on Form SA-1."],[165.6,"N","Have a supervisor observe one session each week with the seven-item checklist, and give the staff member feedback and praise."],[174.2,"O","In the study, staff accuracy rose from 39–74% to 92–97% with feedback, praise and a group reward, and the learners' progress rose with it."],[174.2,"O","The learners were adults: fit the task analysis to your student. The card sets no criterion for moving back a step; the team sets one."],[174.5,"N","In the study, staff accuracy rose from between thirty-nine and seventy-four percent to above ninety percent, and the learners' progress rose with it. The learners were adults, so fit the task analysis to your student. The card sets no criterion for moving back a step. The team sets one."],[195.1,"O","The team decides whether edibles are used. If full physical guidance would be used with a student who resists, complete TD-1's consent block first."],[195.1,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[195.4,"N","The team decides whether edibles are used. If full physical guidance would be used with a student who resists, complete the consent block on Form TD-1 first. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "chain_hw_total": [[0.0,"O","Every step of the chain in every session, with help only on the steps that need it"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Craig, E. A., Dounavi, K., & Ferguson, J. (2021). Telehealth to train interventionists teaching functional living skills to children with autism spectrum disorder. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 54(2), 511–529."],[0.6,"N","Total-task chaining with handwashing: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Craig and colleagues, 2021."],[10.1,"O","The chain: 1. Water on. 2. Wet hands. 3. Soap. 4. Scrub. 5. Rinse. 6. Water off and dry. Baseline: which steps does he do alone?"],[10.4,"N","Write a task analysis fitted to the student. For a baseline, give the instruction, and record which steps he does alone."],[18.8,"O","One short instruction."],[19.1,"N","Start the chain with one short instruction."],[23.0,"Teacher","Wash your hands."],[26.0,"O","Now: step 1, water on. Every step, in order, each session."],[26.3,"N","Teach every step of the chain in every session, in order. The steps he can do alone, he does alone."],[40.5,"O","Now: step 2, wet hands. Every step, in order, each session."],[44.8,"O","Now: step 3, soap. Not done alone: help, most to least."],[45.1,"N","When he does not do a step alone, or starts the wrong step, give help. Start with physical help, and fade to less help as he learns. Use hand-over-hand help only while he accepts it."],[67.3,"O","Now: step 4, scrub. Not done alone: help, most to least."],[73.0,"O","Now: step 5, rinse. Then step back and wait again."],[73.3,"N","On the next step, step back and wait again. He rinses, turns off the water, and dries his hands alone."],[87.8,"O","Now: step 6, water off and dry. Then step back and wait again."],[99.4,"O","The chain: 1. Water on, alone. 2. Wet hands, alone. 3. Soap, prompted. 4. Scrub, prompted. 5. Rinse, alone. 6. Water off and dry, alone. Reinforcement: at the end of the chain only."],[99.7,"N","Give reinforcement only after the last step of the chain."],[105.1,"Teacher","You washed your hands! Great job!"],[109.4,"O","4 of 6 steps alone: 67%."],[109.7,"N","Record each step as independent or prompted while the session runs. Afterward, work out the percentage of steps done independently. Here, four of six: sixty-seven percent."],[123.1,"O","A later session: 1. Water on, alone. 2. Wet hands, alone. 3. Soap. 4. Scrub. 5. Rinse. 6. Water off and dry. Now: step 3, soap. Less help: a gesture."],[123.4,"N","In a later session, he needs less help. A point at the soap is enough, and he scrubs on his own."],[140.8,"O","Now: step 4, scrub. Less help: a gesture."],[146.6,"O","A later session: 1. Water on, alone. 2. Wet hands, alone. 3. Soap, gesture. 4. Scrub, alone. 5. Rinse, alone. 6. Water off and dry, alone. 5 of 6 alone: 83%. Mastered at 80% in 2 sessions in a row."],[146.9,"N","The skill is mastered when eighty percent of the steps are done independently in two sessions in a row."],[153.6,"O","Allow a harmless change of order that the student prefers. After an error, move to the next more intrusive prompt (steps 3 and 4)."],[153.6,"O","Hand-over-hand help is used only while the student accepts it. It stops if the student pulls away."],[153.9,"N","Allow a harmless change of order that the student prefers. After an error, move to the next more intrusive prompt. Hand-over-hand help stops if the student pulls away."],[166.3,"O","Train staff with short written lessons and video sessions with modeling and role play, then coach live until fidelity is 80% on 2 days in a row (step 8)."],[166.3,"O","Data: each step independent or prompted, on Form SA-1; staff fidelity on Form DD-1."],[166.6,"N","To train staff, use short written lessons and video sessions with modeling and role play. Then coach live during the routine, until fidelity reaches eighty percent on two days in a row."],[180.0,"O","In the study, all four students became more independent, but only one met the 80% criterion. Plan for longer teaching, and consider forward or backward chaining if progress is slow."],[180.0,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[180.3,"N","In the study, all four students became more independent, but only one met the eighty percent criterion. So plan for longer teaching, and consider forward or backward chaining if progress is slow. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "token_build": [[0.0,"O","Set out the parts, teach the exchange first, then make each token worth earning"],[0.0,"O","Token economies, a review · degli Espinosa, F., & Hackenberg, T. D. (2024). Token economies: Evidence-based recommendations for practitioners. Behavioral Interventions, 39, e2051."],[0.6,"N","Token economy, part one: building it. A simulated example, after the recommendations of degli Espinosa and Hackenberg, 2024."],[11.8,"O","The parts: Tokens the student can handle. A board to hold them. Backup items and activities. A tray, a shop or a picture menu."],[12.1,"N","A token economy needs tokens, a board to hold them, and backup reinforcers: items and activities found with a preference assessment, and set out on a tray, in a shop, or on a picture menu."],[25.6,"O","The chain, taught from its end: 1. A response earns a token. 2. Tokens fill the board. 3. The board is exchanged. 4. A backup item is chosen. Now: 4. A backup item is chosen."],[25.9,"N","Before any tokens, teach how the tray is reached. She looks at you, with her hands away from the tray. Then you give the signal, and she takes one item."],[43.8,"O","Now: 3. The board is exchanged. In the survey, 6 in 10 left out exchange training."],[44.1,"N","Next, teach the exchange. Set out a full board, and wait. Open your hand only after she has picked the board up. She hands it over, and the tray comes out."],[62.6,"O","Now: 4. A backup item is chosen."],[68.4,"O","Now: 2. Tokens fill the board. First a signal, then a reinforcer."],[68.7,"N","Then set out the board with the last token beside it. She places it, and hands over the board. Placed just before the exchange, the token comes to signal that the backup is near. That is how it becomes a conditioned reinforcer."],[90.8,"O","Now: 1. A response earns a token."],[91.1,"N","From here on, a response earns each token. Hand it to her, and let her place it herself. In one study, children preferred handling their own tokens."],[110.2,"O","Work backward: 4 in place, 1 to earn. 3 in place, 2 to earn. 2 in place, 3 to earn. 1 in place, 4 to earn. All 5 to earn. Now: 3 in place, 2 to earn."],[110.5,"N","Now work backward along the board. Start with four tokens already in place and one to earn. Then three in place and two to earn, and so on, until she fills the whole board. Begin with easy responses, then bring in the skills you are teaching."],[128.0,"O","Test the tokens: Sessions with tokens. Matched sessions without. Clearly more responding with tokens: they work. Now: Clearly more responding with tokens: they work. In the survey, 6 in 10 skipped this test."],[128.3,"N","Before you rely on the tokens, test them. Compare sessions in which responses earn tokens with matched sessions in which they do not. The tokens are working only if she clearly responds more when they are earned. In the survey, six in ten practitioners did not run this check."],[147.0,"O","Start a student who is new to tokens with plain tokens and a simple board. Tokens built on a favourite character are common (70% in a 2023 survey of 255 practitioners) but have very little research behind them."],[147.0,"O","Choose the backup items with a preference assessment, and repeat it often."],[147.3,"N","For a student who is new to tokens, start with plain tokens and a simple board. Tokens built on a favourite character are common, but have very little research behind them. Choose the backup items with a preference assessment, and repeat it often."],[163.4,"O","The exchange is taught first because behavior is strongest closest to the backup reinforcer. Each new step is then reinforced by the step already learned."],[163.4,"O","Pairing each token with a backup item is the other common method. The two have not been compared directly."],[163.7,"N","The exchange is taught first because behavior is strongest closest to the backup reinforcer. Pairing each token with a backup item is the other common method. The two have not been compared directly."],[177.0,"O","On the TD-1 cards, see \"Build new tokens into reinforcers, then check that they work\" (Bonfonte et al., 2020)."],[177.0,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[177.3,"N","On the TD-1 cards, see the card on building new tokens into reinforcers and checking that they work. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "token_schedules": [[0.0,"O","Token production, exchange production and token exchange: what each one sets, and which to change first"],[0.0,"O","Token economies, a review · degli Espinosa, F., & Hackenberg, T. D. (2024). Token economies: Evidence-based recommendations for practitioners. Behavioral Interventions, 39, e2051."],[0.6,"N","Token economy, part two: the three schedules. A simulated example, after the recommendations of degli Espinosa and Hackenberg, 2024."],[12.3,"O","Three schedules: Token production: 2 responses per token. Exchange production: 5 tokens open the exchange. Token exchange: 1 token = 1 minute."],[12.6,"N","Every token economy runs on three schedules. The token-production schedule: how many responses earn a token. The exchange-production schedule: how many tokens open the exchange. And the token-exchange schedule: how many tokens buy one unit of the backup reinforcer."],[32.6,"O","Now: Token production: 2 responses per token."],[32.9,"N","The token-production schedule. Here, two responses earn one token."],[46.8,"O","Now: Exchange production: 5 tokens open the exchange."],[47.1,"N","The exchange-production schedule. Here, five tokens open the exchange: when the board is full, she hands it over."],[67.7,"O","Now: Token exchange: 1 token = 1 minute. Each token buys its own unit."],[68.0,"N","The token-exchange schedule: the price. Here, each token buys one minute with the item she chooses, so five tokens buy five minutes. When every token has its own value, the tokens are worth saving."],[89.5,"O","A second-order schedule: Each response: 1 token. 5 tokens: 1 full board. 3 full boards: the exchange. One schedule built on another."],[89.8,"N","Tokens are earned on one schedule, and the exchange is earned on another, built on the first. So a token economy works like a second-order schedule. It can be stretched further: each full board earns a marker, and three markers open the exchange."],[106.6,"O","Which schedule first?: 6 tokens x 1 response: 6 responses buy 6 units. 3 tokens x 2 responses: 6 responses buy 3 units. Now: 6 tokens x 1 response: 6 responses buy 6 units. Raise exchange production first."],[106.9,"N","To stretch the time between exchanges, raise the exchange-production schedule first, and keep token production low. Six tokens at one response each cost six responses, and buy six units. Three tokens at two responses each cost the same six responses, and buy only three. Raise token production later."],[128.8,"O","Fixed or variable: Fixed: always 5 tokens. Variable: 3, 6, 4, 7, 5 ... about 5. Given the choice, students picked variable. Now: Variable: 3, 6, 4, 7, 5 ... about 5. Argueta et al., 2019."],[129.1,"N","Then move to variable schedules. The number of tokens changes from one exchange to the next, around an average. In a classroom study, students given the choice picked the variable exchange schedule nearly every time. Plan the numbers on a list beforehand."],[147.2,"O","On the TD-1 cards the token-production schedule is listed as the \"unit schedule\", and the exchange-production schedule and prices as the \"exchange schedule\"."],[147.2,"O","Raise a schedule in small steps, and only while responding holds steady (Bonfonte et al., 2020: one response at a time, after 3 steady sessions)."],[147.5,"N","On the TD-1 cards, the token-production schedule is listed as the unit schedule, and the exchange-production schedule and prices as the exchange schedule. Raise a schedule in small steps, and only while responding holds steady."],[163.3,"O","Let each token buy its own unit of the backup: one token, one minute. In the survey, 48% gave a set time whatever the number of tokens, and 12% never exchanged tokens at all: then the board is only a progress bar."],[163.3,"O","If responding stops after a schedule is raised (ratio strain), go back to the last step that worked."],[163.6,"N","Let each token buy its own unit of the backup. In a survey of practitioners, nearly half gave a set time whatever the number of tokens, and about one in eight never exchanged tokens at all. Then the board is only a progress bar. If responding stops after a schedule is raised, go back to the last step that worked."],[185.8,"O","Variable schedules need planning: write the numbers on a list beforehand, and bring out a different-looking exchange token when the requirement is met."],[185.8,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[186.1,"N","Variable schedules need planning: write the numbers on a list beforehand. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "token_running": [[0.0,"O","A wide choice at the exchange, an economy closed as far as you can, and where response cost fits"],[0.0,"O","Token economies, a review · degli Espinosa, F., & Hackenberg, T. D. (2024). Token economies: Evidence-based recommendations for practitioners. Behavioral Interventions, 39, e2051."],[0.6,"N","Token economy, part three: running it. A simulated example, after the recommendations of degli Espinosa and Hackenberg, 2024."],[11.9,"O","What a token buys: One backup: a specific token. Many backups: a generalized token. Holds its value when one item loses appeal. Now: Many backups: a generalized token."],[12.2,"N","A token that buys many different things is a generalized reinforcer. It holds its value even when she is tired of one item. So offer a wide range of backup items and activities, and check them often with a preference assessment."],[27.4,"O","When to choose: Before the session: one item, fixed. At the exchange: the full choice. Now: At the exchange: the full choice. In the survey, 56% chose before the session."],[27.7,"N","Let her choose at the exchange, from everything on offer, and not before the session. What she wants at the start may not be what she wants at the end. In the survey, more than half had the student pick beforehand."],[48.0,"O","Now: At the exchange: the full choice."],[53.8,"O","Open or closed: Closed: backups only through tokens. Open: also free at other times. Closed economies give stronger responding. Now: Closed: backups only through tokens. Hursh, 1980; Roane et al., 2005."],[54.1,"N","In a closed economy, the backup reinforcers are available only by earning them. In an open economy, they are also free at other times. When an item is free elsewhere, it is worth less here. Studies outside token systems found stronger responding in closed economies."],[73.8,"O","Closing the economy: Backups only through the tokens. Items found only in this setting. Or a fuller version here than elsewhere."],[74.1,"N","So close the economy as far as you can. Keep the backup items for the token exchange. Use items and activities found only in this setting. Where full restriction is not possible, offer a fuller version here: the tablet with the internet at school, without it elsewhere."],[92.6,"O","Every task inside: New skills: a token for every response. Mastered skills: a leaner schedule. No task pays outside the tokens."],[92.9,"N","Closing the economy also means that all teaching earns tokens. If some tasks pay with tokens and others pay with an item straight away, the token tasks lose. Keep every task inside the token system, and set the schedule to suit each task."],[118.1,"O","Response cost: A token is taken after the target behavior. A punishment procedure. Restrictive: consent and approval first. Now: Restrictive: consent and approval first. Approved on Form TE-1 before use."],[118.4,"N","Response cost means taking a token away after the problem behavior. It is a punishment procedure. On Form TD-1 it is marked restrictive, and it needs consent and approval before use. Show the student the rule beforehand, make sure she can keep a positive balance, and watch for frustration."],[145.8,"O","When responding drops: Does she exchange without help? Do the tokens still work? Test them. Are the backups still wanted? Was a schedule raised too fast?"],[146.1,"N","When responding drops, check each part in turn. Does she still exchange without help? Do the tokens still work when you test them? Are the backup items still wanted? Was a schedule raised too fast? Fix the part that failed; do not rebuild the whole system."],[164.5,"O","Pricing items differently has no research yet to guide it. If you do, make sure enough tokens can be saved to reach the dearer items."],[164.5,"O","Check the backups often with a preference assessment, and keep a favourite item on most exchanges, not all, so other items are sampled."],[164.8,"N","Pricing items differently has no research yet to guide it. If you do, make sure enough tokens can be saved to reach the dearer items. Keep a favourite item on most exchanges, not all, so that other items are sampled."],[180.1,"O","Response cost is a punishment procedure. On Form TD-1 it is marked restrictive: it needs consent and approval before use, and the student is shown the rule beforehand."],[180.1,"O","Make sure the student can keep a positive balance, and watch for frustration as tokens are lost (TD-1 card after Rapport et al., 1982)."],[180.4,"N","Response cost is a punishment procedure. It is restrictive, and needs consent and approval before use. Make sure the student can keep a positive balance, and watch for frustration as tokens are lost."],[194.0,"O","When a token economy is no longer needed, most practitioners in the survey end it by thinning the schedules step by step."],[194.0,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[194.3,"N","When a token economy is no longer needed, most practitioners in the survey end it by thinning the schedules step by step. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "prompt_ltm": [[0.0,"O","The least help first: the instruction alone, then a model, then gentle guidance"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Sy, J. R., Donaldson, J. M., Vollmer, T. R., & Pizarro, E. (2014). An evaluation of factors that influence children’s instruction following. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 47(1), 101–112."],[0.6,"N","Least-to-most prompting with one-step instructions: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Sy and colleagues, 2014."],[10.8,"O","Least to most: 1. The instruction alone: 10 s. 2. A model: 5 s to imitate. 3. Gentle manual guidance. Baseline first: no prompts."],[11.1,"N","First, list one-step instructions the student does not yet follow, and confirm she has the motor skills for each. Take a baseline with no prompts. In this example the instruction is: put the cup on the plate."],[26.5,"O","Now: 1. The instruction alone: 10 s."],[26.8,"N","Give the instruction once, and wait ten seconds."],[31.1,"Teacher","Put the cup on the plate."],[35.8,"O","Now: 1. The instruction alone: 10 s."],[36.1,"N","If she does the action before doing anything else, give praise and her chosen reinforcer."],[49.2,"O","Now: 1. The instruction alone: 10 s. Alone: praise and her chosen item."],[49.7,"Teacher","You put the cup on the plate! Nice work!"],[59.5,"O","Now: 2. A model: 5 s to imitate."],[59.8,"N","On another trial, she does not respond, or responds incorrectly. Model the action, and wait five seconds."],[75.3,"O","Now: 2. A model: 5 s to imitate. After a model: praise only."],[75.9,"N","She imitates, so give praise only."],[81.0,"O","Now: 3. Gentle manual guidance."],[81.3,"N","If she does not imitate, gently guide her hands through the action, and give praise. Stop the guidance if she pulls away."],[89.9,"O","Now: 3. Gentle manual guidance. After guidance: praise only."],[96.5,"O","The check: Its own objects only. Extra objects on the table. Two instructions, same objects. Now: Extra objects on the table."],[96.8,"N","Once the instruction is mastered with only its own objects on the table, check it. Alternate trials with only those objects, and trials with another instruction's objects also on the table. In the study, correct responding then fell to about half."],[113.6,"O","Now: Two instructions, same objects."],[113.9,"N","Then add trials in which two instructions use the same objects: cup on plate, and plate on cup. Now only the words tell her which action is right."],[125.3,"O","Now: Two instructions, same objects. Here: the plate on the cup, an error."],[130.5,"O","Mastery: 100% in 3 sessions in a row."],[130.8,"N","Count an instruction as mastered only at one hundred percent correct across three sessions in a row, in these mixed arrangements. If accuracy stays near fifty percent, plan added discrimination teaching."],[144.8,"O","The reinforcement rule: an independent correct response earns praise and the chosen reinforcer; a response after the model or after guidance earns praise only (steps 3 to 5)."],[144.8,"O","Data: each trial independent, prompted or incorrect, for each arrangement, on Form SA-1."],[145.1,"N","Keep the reinforcement rule: praise and the chosen reinforcer for an independent response, and praise only after a model or after guidance. Record each trial as independent, prompted, or incorrect."],[160.3,"O","In the study, all three children learned object instructions with only the relevant objects on the table. With extra objects there, they were right about half the time."],[160.3,"O","The lesson: prompting and reinforcement alone did not bring the actions under the control of the spoken words. Use the mixed arrangements as a check on any instruction-following program."],[160.6,"N","In the study, all three children learned object instructions when only the relevant objects were on the table. With extra objects there, they were right about half the time. So prompting and reinforcement alone did not bring the actions under the control of the spoken words. Use the mixed arrangements as a check."],[181.2,"O","Manual guidance is a prompt the student accepts: it stops if she pulls away. The team decides whether food is used as a reinforcer."],[181.2,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[181.5,"N","Manual guidance is a prompt the student accepts. It stops if she pulls away. The team decides whether food is used as a reinforcer. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "prompt_mtl": [[0.0,"O","Start with the help that works every time, and fade it as she succeeds"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Cengher, M., Shamoun, K., Moss, P., Roll, D., Feliciano, G., & Fienup, D. M. (2016). A comparison of the effects of two prompt-fading strategies on skill acquisition in children with autism spectrum disorders. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 9(2), 115–125."],[0.6,"N","Most-to-least prompting with one-step directions: a simulated example of the TD-1 card after Cengher and colleagues, 2016."],[10.8,"O","Prompt-type test: A model. A gesture. A partial physical prompt. A full physical prompt. Keep the type that works every time."],[11.1,"N","Before teaching, test each prompt type: a model, a gesture, a partial physical prompt, and a full physical prompt. Keep only the type that produces a correct response every time. In the study, that was the physical prompts."],[28.1,"O","She picks what to work for."],[28.4,"N","Have the student pick what she will work for in this session."],[32.3,"O","Most to least: 1. Full: hand over hand. 2. Heavier partial physical. 3. Light partial physical. 4. Alone, within 2 s."],[32.6,"N","Get her attention, then give the direction once."],[36.9,"Teacher","Put the cup on the plate."],[40.8,"O","Now: 1. Full: hand over hand."],[41.1,"N","Wait two seconds. If she has not responded, give the prompt at the current level. Start with full hand-over-hand guidance."],[56.8,"O","Now: 2. Heavier partial physical. 2 correct in a row: the next lighter prompt."],[57.1,"N","After two correct trials in a row at a level, move to the next lighter prompt. Here, a heavier partial prompt: a hand on her forearm."],[74.3,"O","Now: 3. Light partial physical. 2 correct in a row: the next lighter prompt."],[74.6,"N","After two more, lighter again. Here, a light touch on her arm."],[86.6,"O","Now: 3. Light partial physical. Reinforce at or below the lightest level that has worked."],[86.9,"N","Reinforce only correct responses at, or below, the lightest prompt that has already worked. A response that needed more help than that earns no reinforcer."],[98.8,"Teacher","You put the cup on the plate! Nice work!"],[108.6,"O","Now: 4. Alone, within 2 s."],[108.9,"N","Keep fading, until she follows the direction alone, within two seconds."],[120.3,"O","Now: 4. Alone, within 2 s. Mastery: 90% alone, 2 sessions in a row."],[120.6,"N","A direction is mastered at ninety percent or more independent, for two sessions in a row. Then probe it once in every five teaching sessions."],[131.0,"O","Run 10-trial sessions: two target directions, 5 trials each, in random order (step 7)."],[131.0,"O","Data: the percentage of trials with an independent correct response within 2 s, and the prompt level on each trial, on Form DD-1."],[131.3,"N","Run ten-trial sessions, with two target directions, five trials each, in random order. Record the prompt level on every trial, and the percentage of trials done alone within two seconds."],[144.5,"O","In the study, all three children mastered the directions taught most-to-least in 24 to 34 sessions. None mastered the directions taught least-to-most in 29 to 42 sessions."],[144.5,"O","Assess prompt types early: a history of prompts that do not work appeared to slow later learning. Maintenance was variable, so plan for it."],[144.8,"N","In the study, all three children mastered the directions taught most-to-least. None mastered the directions taught least-to-most. So assess prompt types early. A history of prompts that do not work appeared to slow later learning. Maintenance was variable, so plan for it."],[165.1,"O","Use physical prompts only with family consent, and when the student accepts touch. The team decides whether food is used as a reinforcer."],[165.1,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[165.4,"N","Use physical prompts only with family consent, and when the student accepts touch. The team decides whether food is used as a reinforcer. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "prompt_delay": [[0.0,"O","The prompt comes at once at first; then a wait gives her the chance to respond before it"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Striefel, S., Bryan, K. S., & Aikins, D. A. (1974). Transfer of stimulus control from motor to verbal stimuli. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 7(1), 123–135. McKeown, C. A., Walker, S. F., & Peters, K. P. (2025). Comparison of blocked versus mixed trialing when teaching foundational skills to early learners. Behavioral Interventions, 40(1), e2071."],[0.6,"N","Time delay with one-step instructions: a simulated example of two TD-1 cards, after Striefel and colleagues, 1974, and McKeown and colleagues, 2025."],[13.8,"O","Time delay: 1. The instruction. 2. The wait: the delay. 3. The prompt: a model. Now: 3. The prompt: a model. The prompt must work every time."],[14.1,"N","Choose a controlling prompt: one that already produces the correct response every time. Here it is a model, because she already imitates the action."],[24.6,"O","The delay: 0 s: the prompt at once. Constant: 5 s every time. Progressive: 0.5 s, 1 s, 2 s ... Now: 0 s: the prompt at once."],[24.9,"N","Start at zero delay. Give the instruction, and the model at once. Reinforce the prompted response."],[33.2,"Teacher","Put the cup on the plate."],[47.5,"O","Now: Constant: 5 s every time."],[47.8,"N","With a constant delay, stay at zero delay until she is at one hundred percent for two sessions. Then wait up to five seconds after the instruction, every time."],[59.6,"O","Now: Constant: 5 s every time. Wait up to 5 s."],[60.0,"Teacher","Put the cup on the plate."],[64.8,"O","Now: Constant: 5 s every time. Before the prompt: no model."],[65.1,"N","If she responds before the prompt, do not model. Reinforce at once, with praise and her chosen item."],[79.0,"O","Now: Constant: 5 s every time. Before the prompt: praise and her item."],[79.5,"Teacher","You put the cup on the plate! Nice work!"],[89.3,"O","Now: Constant: 5 s every time. Error or none: repeat, prompt, reinforce."],[89.6,"N","After an error or no response, repeat the instruction, prompt the correct response, and reinforce. If correct responding starts to fall, stop reinforcing the prompted responses."],[109.0,"O","Now: Progressive: 0.5 s, 1 s, 2 s ... After an error: back one step."],[109.3,"N","With a progressive delay, the wait grows instead. After the zero-delay trial, wait about half a second, then one second, then one second longer after each correct response. After an error, go back one step."],[125.8,"Teacher","Put the cup on the plate."],[134.5,"O","Now: Progressive: 0.5 s, 1 s, 2 s ... Learned: 14 of 15 across 3 mixed sets."],[134.8,"N","When she responds before the model five times in a row, mix the new instruction with ones she already follows. Count it as learned at fourteen of fifteen correct across three mixed sets."],[147.6,"O","Constant delay (McKeown et al., 2025): mastery at 91% or more in two sessions in a row, with a correct first trial of each target."],[147.6,"O","Progressive delay (Striefel et al., 1974): four objects on the table on every trial, so the student must choose."],[147.9,"N","For the constant delay, the study's mastery criterion was ninety-one percent in two sessions in a row. For the progressive delay, four objects were on the table on every trial, so the student had to choose."],[162.2,"O","In the 1974 study, control usually moved from the model to the spoken instruction on the first delay trial. Untaught instructions did not improve: each one had to be taught."],[162.2,"O","In the 2025 study, mixed trials worked about as well as blocked trials for early learners."],[162.5,"N","In the older study, control usually moved from the model to the spoken instruction on the first delay trial. But untaught instructions did not improve, so each one had to be taught. In the newer study, mixed trials worked about as well as blocked trials."],[180.4,"O","After an error, the 1974 study used a spoken \"no\" and full physical guidance. Use a neutral error signal instead, and complete TD-1's consent block before guiding a student through a response."],[180.4,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[180.7,"N","After an error, the older study used a spoken no, and full physical guidance. Use a neutral error signal instead, and complete the consent block on Form TD-1 before guiding a student through a response. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "ext_wait": [[0.0,"O","The protest does not end the work. The work waits, and nothing else is offered"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Ward, S., Parker, A., & Perdikaris, A. (2017). Task as reinforcer: A reactive alternative to traditional forms of escape extinction. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 10(1), 22–34."],[0.6,"N","Escape extinction with no physical guidance: the wait out. A simulated example of the TD-1 card after Ward, Parker and Perdikaris, 2017."],[12.8,"O","Wait out: 1. \"The work will wait\": slide it away. 2. Turn away; no reinforcers. 3. Calm 5 s, looking 2 s: \"Ready?\". 4. Ready: work and attention back. First: work she can do, and reinforcement."],[13.1,"N","Keep the work at the student's level, with a mix of mastered and new tasks, demand fading, and reinforcement for cooperating."],[28.5,"O","Now: 1. \"The work will wait\": slide it away."],[28.8,"N","When she protests, whines, argues, or does not respond within five seconds, say briefly that the work will wait, and slide the materials, and her token strip if she has one, about a foot away."],[49.3,"Teacher","The work will wait."],[55.5,"O","Now: 2. Turn away; no reinforcers."],[55.8,"N","Turn away. Give no attention and no other reinforcers while she stays at the table."],[62.2,"O","Now: 3. Calm 5 s, looking 2 s: \"Ready?\"."],[62.5,"N","Wait for at least five seconds of calm, with two seconds of looking toward you. Then move the materials slightly closer, and ask whether she is ready."],[82.0,"Teacher","Are you ready?"],[85.7,"O","Now: 4. Ready: work and attention back."],[86.0,"N","When she signals that she is ready, in any way, return the materials and your attention. Give no extra reinforcers for coming back."],[100.4,"O","Now: 2. Turn away; no reinforcers. Not ready: back to step 2."],[100.7,"N","If she escalates, or shows no readiness within five seconds, turn away, and move the materials away again."],[108.7,"O","A polite request keeps the work in place."],[109.0,"N","For aggression to a known trigger, end the trigger as usual, but also run a wait out. A polite request, such as: please don't touch that, ends the trigger and keeps the work in place."],[122.4,"O","Record: how many, and how long."],[122.7,"N","Record the number and the length of each wait out, with a count-up timer."],[127.8,"O","What to expect: wait outs are long at first. In the study, one ran for more than 18 minutes on day 3; over the next 2 weeks there were five brief ones."],[127.8,"O","Data: the number and the length of wait outs each day, with a count-up timer (step 8)."],[128.1,"N","Expect long wait outs at first. In the study, one ran for more than eighteen minutes on day three. Over the next two weeks there were five brief ones. Record the number and the length of wait outs each day."],[143.3,"O","A wait out is time-out from instruction, and it is restrictive: it needs review and consent under district rules. Complete the escape-extinction consent block on Form TD-1 first."],[143.3,"O","Do not add physical blocking. If she cannot stay seated, the authors suggest letting her leave the area while access to reinforcers stays limited."],[143.6,"N","A wait out is time-out from instruction, and it is restrictive. It needs review and consent under district rules, so complete the escape-extinction consent block on Form TD-1 first. Do not add physical blocking. If she cannot stay seated, the authors suggest letting her leave the area, while access to reinforcers stays limited."],[167.5,"O","The evidence is preliminary: three small studies without a baseline phase. Use it only where the function is escape, with reinforcement for cooperating."],[167.5,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[167.8,"N","The evidence is preliminary: three small studies, without a baseline phase. Use it only where the function is escape, together with reinforcement for cooperating. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "ext_ignore": [[0.0,"O","The whining no longer brings an answer. Every adult responds the same way"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Hall, R. V., Axelrod, S., Tyler, L., Grief, E., Jones, F. C., & Robertson, R. (1972). Modification of behavior problems in the home with a parent as observer and experimenter. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 5(1), 53–64."],[0.6,"N","Planned ignoring of whining that adults had been answering. A simulated example of the TD-1 card after Hall and colleagues, 1972."],[11.3,"O","Planned ignoring: 1. Turn away without comment. 2. Keep doing another activity. 3. No comfort, arguing or \"stop\". 4. Every adult the same, all day. First: a definition and a tally."],[11.6,"N","First, agree on a definition of whining and shouting: speech pitched or loud enough that you would call it a whine or a shout. Post tally sheets where adults can mark each instance."],[24.8,"O","Baseline: the whining is answered."],[25.1,"N","Record a baseline for about two weeks, and note how adults respond now. In the study, the parents had been comforting the child, or telling him to stop."],[39.2,"Teacher","Oh, what's wrong? It's okay."],[42.3,"O","Now: 1. Turn away without comment."],[42.6,"N","Now, when she whines or shouts, turn away without comment, and keep doing another activity."],[49.4,"O","Now: 2. Keep doing another activity."],[56.0,"O","Now: 2. Keep doing another activity. If safe: step out until it stops."],[56.3,"N","If it is safe, and another adult is supervising, move out of the area until the whining stops."],[63.4,"O","Now: 3. No comfort, arguing or \"stop\"."],[63.8,"N","Do not comfort her, argue with her, or tell her to stop during the episode."],[70.0,"O","Now: 4. Every adult the same, all day."],[70.3,"N","Make sure every adult who is with her responds the same way, all day. In the study, the mother covered the day, and the father the evening."],[80.0,"O","Some days: two adults tally."],[80.3,"N","On some days, have two adults tally at the same time, to check their agreement."],[85.8,"O","Keep it in place; recheck later."],[86.2,"N","After the rate is low, keep the response in place, and recheck on single days later."],[93.3,"O","In the study, whining and shouting fell from 10.2 a day to 4.6 when the parents ignored it, rose to 8.7 when they answered it again, and fell to 2.8 in the second ignoring phase."],[93.3,"O","Data: a count of whines and shouts each day, tallied by each adult on duty (step 1)."],[93.6,"N","In the study, whining and shouting fell from ten point two a day to four point six when the parents ignored it. It rose to eight point seven when they answered it again, and fell to two point eight in the second ignoring phase. Tally each instance, every day."],[111.5,"O","The study used ignoring alone. For a student on your caseload, pair it with attention for calm speech, or a taught request, and plan for a burst before starting."],[111.5,"O","Do not ignore behavior that is unsafe."],[111.8,"N","The study used ignoring alone. For a student on your caseload, pair it with attention for calm speech, or a taught request, and plan for a burst before starting. Do not ignore behavior that is unsafe."],[126.8,"O","The evidence is one preschool child at home, with his parents as observers; the function was inferred from the reversal, not from a functional analysis."],[126.8,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[127.1,"N","The evidence is one preschool child at home, with his parents as observers. The function was inferred from the reversal, not from a functional analysis. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "ext_kind": [[0.0,"O","At a tantrum, comfort and validation at once. The item waits for a calm response"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Tarbox, C., Tarbox, J., Bermudez, T. L., Silverman, E., & Servellon, L. (2026). Kind extinction: A procedural variation on traditional extinction. Behavior Analysis in Practice, 19(1), 88–98."],[0.6,"N","Kind extinction: comfort, but not the item or the break, when tantrums occur during teaching. A simulated example of the TD-1 card after Tarbox and colleagues, 2026."],[14.8,"O","Kind extinction: 1. A calm request or response: the item. 2. A tantrum: kindness at once. 3. Not the item, not the break. 4. Next instruction; item when calm. First: escape or items, not attention."],[15.1,"N","First, confirm with a functional analysis, or a practical functional assessment, that the behavior is maintained by escape, by access to items, or both, and not by attention."],[27.9,"O","Her choice for this block: the tablet."],[28.2,"N","Before each five to ten minute teaching block, give a quick choice between two or three highly preferred items. These are the items she earns. Here, she chose the tablet."],[41.0,"O","Teach as usual: no physical prompts."],[41.5,"N","Teach as usual, with her own prompting hierarchy, but never physically prompt compliance."],[54.1,"O","Now: 1. A calm request or response: the item."],[54.4,"N","When she responds to an instruction, or asks for the item or a break, without problem behavior, give thirty seconds with the item, right away."],[64.0,"O","Now: 1. A calm request or response: the item. 30 s with the item, right away."],[70.6,"O","Now: 2. A tantrum: kindness at once."],[70.9,"N","When a tantrum or a protest starts, respond at once with her chosen act of kindness. Here, the adult offers a hand, and says that the task feels hard. Do not give the break, or the item."],[91.6,"O","Now: 3. Not the item, not the break."],[92.7,"Teacher","This feels hard. I can see you're frustrated."],[98.2,"O","Now: 4. Next instruction; item when calm."],[98.5,"N","After the comfort, present the next instruction, with the same non-physical prompts. If she leaves the area, bring the materials to her."],[108.6,"O","Now: 4. Next instruction; item when calm."],[108.9,"N","Keep the item withheld until she makes a replacement response, a request or a response to an instruction, with no problem behavior. Then give it."],[119.4,"O","Now: 4. Next instruction; item when calm. A calm response: now the item."],[126.0,"O","Kindness is offered, never forced."],[126.3,"N","Offer acts of kindness; never force them. A hug is offered, not given. If an act makes her frown, grimace, or move away, choose a different one for the rest of the session."],[139.5,"O","In the study, tantrums fell from 0.47 to 1.25 a minute in baseline to 0.13 to 0.22 a minute (62 to 88% lower), and to 0 to 0.07 a minute at the 2 to 4 week follow-up."],[139.5,"O","Requests rose for all four children. Data: tantrums and requests per minute on Form DD-1; responses to instructions on Form SA-1."],[139.8,"N","In the study, tantrums fell by sixty-two to eighty-eight percent, and by ninety-four to one hundred percent at the two to four week follow-up. Requests rose for all four children. Count tantrums and requests each session."],[155.9,"O","Attention follows the problem behavior here, so this is only for behavior shown not to be maintained by attention (step 1). Watch the data, and stop if the rate does not fall."],[155.9,"O","Escape extinction is in use: complete the escape-extinction consent block on Form TD-1 first."],[156.2,"N","Attention follows the problem behavior here. So this is only for behavior shown not to be maintained by attention. Watch the data, and stop if the rate does not fall. Escape extinction is in use, so complete the escape-extinction consent block on Form TD-1 first."],[175.8,"O","Once the rate falls, train the rest of the team the same way, and recheck at 2 to 4 weeks. The evidence is a four-child program evaluation, with no comparison to standard extinction."],[175.8,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[176.1,"N","Once the rate falls, train the rest of the team the same way, and recheck at two to four weeks. The evidence is a four-child program evaluation, with no comparison to standard extinction. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "ante_fade": [[0.0,"O","Start with no instructions. Add them one step at a time, with a break for each one he completes"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Ringdahl, J. E., Kitsukawa, K., Andelman, M. S., Call, N., Winborn, L., Barretto, A., & Reed, G. K. (2002). Differential reinforcement with and without instructional fading. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 35(3), 291–294."],[0.6,"N","Demand fading: instructions added back slowly, with a break for each one he completes. A simulated example of the TD-1 card after Ringdahl and colleagues, 2002."],[13.8,"O","Demand fading: 1. No instructions at first. 2. Then 1 every 15 min. 3. Done alone: a 1-min break. 4. Clean session: add 1 more. First: escape from instructions, confirmed."],[14.1,"N","First, confirm that the problem behavior is maintained by escape from instructions."],[19.9,"O","Now: 1. No instructions at first."],[20.2,"N","Run the first three sessions, of forty-five minutes each, with leisure items available, and no instructions at all."],[34.5,"O","Now: 2. Then 1 every 15 min."],[34.8,"N","Then begin with one academic instruction every fifteen minutes: a spelling card, a matching task, or counting."],[45.4,"Teacher","Write the word: sun."],[49.8,"O","Now: 3. Done alone: a 1-min break."],[50.1,"N","When he completes the instruction alone, with no problem behavior, give a one-minute break with the leisure items."],[64.0,"O","Now: 3. Done alone: a 1-min break. 1 minute with the blocks."],[70.6,"O","Problem behavior: the next instruction."],[70.9,"N","If problem behavior occurs during an instruction, give the next instruction, and keep going. If materials were thrown or torn, prompt him to put them back."],[90.9,"Teacher","Now write the word: hat."],[94.9,"O","Now: 4. Clean session: add 1 more."],[95.2,"N","After each forty-five minute session with no problem behavior, add one more instruction per fifteen minutes."],[102.6,"O","Now: 4. Clean session: add 1 more. Goal: about 1 every other minute."],[102.9,"N","Stop adding instructions when the rate reaches the classroom rate: about one instruction every other minute."],[110.0,"O","Record: instructions and problem behavior."],[110.3,"N","Record each instruction given, and each episode of problem behavior, in every session."],[116.5,"O","In the study, with instructions faded in, problem behavior occurred in only 2 sessions and averaged 0 in the final 3 sessions, at the classroom rate of 0.5 instructions a minute."],[116.5,"O","With the same break at the full rate from the start, problem behavior was high at first and fell to 0.2 a minute in the final 3 sessions."],[116.8,"N","In the study, with instructions faded in, problem behavior occurred in only two sessions, and averaged zero in the final three sessions, at the classroom rate. With the same break, but the full rate from the start, problem behavior was high at first, and fell only later."],[135.5,"O","Giving the next instruction after problem behavior is escape extinction: complete the escape-extinction consent block on Form TD-1 first."],[135.5,"O","The study also ended the break when problem behavior occurred in it. That is a punishment procedure and is restrictive: it is not shown here, and the other steps can be used without it."],[135.8,"N","Giving the next instruction after problem behavior is escape extinction, so complete the escape-extinction consent block on Form TD-1 first. The study also ended the break when problem behavior occurred in it. That is a punishment procedure, and it is restrictive. It is not shown here, and the other steps can be used without it."],[158.0,"O","The evidence is one student, and each condition ran in its own room with different staff, so the setting may explain part of the difference."],[158.0,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[158.3,"N","The evidence is one student, and each condition ran in its own room, with different staff, so the setting may explain part of the difference. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "ante_task": [[0.0,"O","Find the task feature that sets off escape behavior, and change how that work is taught"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · McComas, J., Hoch, H., Paone, D., & El-Roy, D. (2000). Escape behavior during academic tasks: A preliminary analysis of idiosyncratic establishing operations. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 33(4), 479–493."],[0.6,"N","Changing the task, so that escape is less valuable: a strategy aid for hard work. A simulated example of the TD-1 card after McComas and colleagues, 2000."],[13.5,"O","Changing the task: 1. Find the task feature. 2. Hard work: an aid on the table. 3. No start in 10 s: read and point. 4. Praise about every 5th response. Now: 1. Find the task feature."],[13.8,"N","First, confirm escape with a brief functional analysis. Then watch his daily work, note what happens before and after each incident, and ask his teacher and parents which kinds of work lead to problem behavior."],[35.6,"O","Now: 1. Find the task feature. Here: the addition is too hard."],[35.9,"N","Write one hypothesis about the task feature that makes escape valuable, such as work that is too hard, or work that is repeated. And write one change in how that work is taught."],[49.2,"O","Test: sessions with it and without it."],[49.5,"N","Test the change by alternating sessions with it and without it, on the same tasks, keeping everything else the same."],[57.5,"O","Now: 2. Hard work: an aid on the table."],[57.8,"N","For hard work, put a strategy aid on the table: here, counters for addition. Then give the instruction."],[68.3,"Teacher","Do your work."],[78.5,"O","Now: 3. No start in 10 s: read and point."],[78.8,"N","If he does not start within ten seconds, read the problem aloud, and point to it."],[85.6,"Teacher","Six plus five."],[87.3,"O","Now: 3. No start in 10 s: read and point. Then: point to the aid."],[88.2,"N","If, after ten more seconds, he still has not started, point to the aid."],[97.7,"O","Repeated work: a new worksheet."],[98.0,"N","For repeated work, give a new worksheet on the same skill, instead of having him redo one he has already completed."],[112.4,"O","Now: 4. Praise about every 5th response."],[112.7,"N","Praise working on about every fifth response. Later, probe the change with a new instructor, in a new room, and with new materials, at one month, and at one year."],[127.6,"Teacher","Nice working."],[131.5,"O","In the study, with counters on the table, problem behavior stayed near zero, against 20% to 40% of intervals without them. Compliance averaged 81% against 34%, and problems correct rose from 0.125 to 2.75 a minute."],[131.5,"O","The effect held at 1 month and at 1 year, with new people, a new room and new tasks. Problem behavior returned when the aid was removed."],[131.8,"N","In the study, with counters on the table, problem behavior stayed near zero, against twenty to forty percent of intervals without them. Compliance averaged eighty-one percent, against thirty-four. The effect held at one month and at one year, and problem behavior returned when the aid was removed."],[152.2,"O","The study also physically helped the student work the problem when he still had not started. That is physical guidance through a demand and is restrictive: it is not shown here."],[152.2,"O","In the study a short break still followed every problem behavior. In school, pair this change with a taught break request and the consequences in the student's plan."],[152.5,"N","The study also physically helped the student work the problem, when he still had not started. That is physical guidance through a demand, and it is restrictive. It is not shown here. In the study, a short break still followed every problem behavior. In school, pair this change with a taught break request, and the consequences in his plan."],[175.8,"O","The evidence is three students, with few sessions in some conditions."],[175.8,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[176.1,"N","The evidence is three students, with few sessions in some conditions. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "ante_mix": [[0.0,"O","Three quick, easy problems lead into each hard one, so the hard one is started sooner"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Lee, D. L., Lylo, B., Vostal, B., & Hua, Y. (2012). The effects of high-preference problems on the completion of nonpreferred mathematics problems. Journal of Applied Behavior Analysis, 45(1), 223–228."],[0.6,"N","Interspersal: three easy problems before each hard one, on the worksheet itself. A simulated example of the TD-1 card after Lee and colleagues, 2012."],[13.0,"O","Interspersal: 1. Each row: 3 easy facts, then 1 hard problem. 2. More than he can finish. 3. 10 min; move back, no comments. 4. Stop, collect, thank him. First: does he choose the easy sheet?"],[13.3,"N","First, confirm the preference. Over five trials, let him choose between a sheet of short, easy facts, and a sheet of a few long problems."],[23.9,"O","Easy items: only facts he gets right."],[24.2,"N","Remove from the easy items any facts that he often misses."],[28.8,"O","Now: 1. Each row: 3 easy facts, then 1 hard problem. A row: 3 x 4   6 x 2   5 x 5   348 x 276."],[29.1,"N","Build each page with five rows. Each row has three single-digit facts, followed by one multidigit problem that he can already do."],[38.9,"O","Now: 2. More than he can finish."],[39.2,"N","Hand him a packet with more problems than can be finished, and ask him to do as many as possible, row by row, until he is told to stop."],[48.5,"Teacher","Do as many as you can, row by row, until I say stop."],[54.9,"O","Now: 3. 10 min; move back, no comments."],[55.2,"N","Start a ten-minute timer, and move back. In the study, the adult stood about three meters behind the student."],[69.8,"O","Now: 3. 10 min; move back, no comments."],[70.1,"N","Watch that he works row by row, and do not comment on the work."],[75.0,"O","Now: 1. Each row: 3 easy facts, then 1 hard problem. Three easy facts: quick answers."],[81.6,"O","Now: 1. Each row: 3 easy facts, then 1 hard problem. Then the hard problem, started at once."],[88.2,"O","Now: 4. Stop, collect, thank him."],[88.5,"N","At ten minutes, ask him to stop, collect the packet, and thank him for working."],[95.9,"Teacher","Please stop. Thank you for working."],[101.0,"O","Score: start time, % correct, digits a minute."],[101.3,"N","Then score the time he took to start each hard problem, the percentage of problems correct, and the digits correct per minute."],[109.9,"O","In the study, the mean time to start a hard problem fell from 7.0 to 3.8 s, from 3.9 to 2.6 s and from 4.8 to 2.5 s for the three students."],[109.9,"O","Accuracy changed only a little (93% to 91%, 57% to 63%, 63% to 75%), and digits correct per minute barely moved."],[110.2,"N","In the study, the mean time to start a hard problem fell from seven seconds to under four, for the slowest student, and by a third or more for the other two. Accuracy changed only a little, and digits correct per minute barely moved."],[126.4,"O","Use it for independent practice of skills the student already has, not for teaching new ones. There was no praise or points during work: finishing problems was the only reinforcer."],[126.4,"O","If refusal continues, assess its function, and pair this with a taught break or help request."],[126.7,"N","Use it for independent practice of skills he already has, not for teaching new ones. There was no praise, and there were no points, during work: finishing problems was the only reinforcer. If refusal continues, assess its function, and pair this with a taught break or help request."],[146.7,"O","The evidence is three adolescents in one alternative school, with 10-minute sessions and no function assessment. Maintenance was not measured."],[146.7,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[147.0,"N","The evidence is three adolescents in one alternative school, with ten-minute sessions, and no function assessment. Maintenance was not measured. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "calm_breath": [[0.0,"O","Teach slow belly breathing when she is calm. Then prompt it at the first signs of aggression, and praise it"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Phillips, L. A., Fritz, J. N., Rettig, L. A., & Martin, K. E. (2019). Diaphragmatic breathing as treatment for escape-maintained aggression. Behavioral Interventions, 34(2), 216–230."],[0.6,"N","Belly breathing, prompted at the first signs of aggression. A simulated example of the TD-1 card after Phillips and colleagues, 2019."],[11.4,"O","Belly breathing: 1. Teach it when calm. 2. Watch for the first signs. 3. At a sign: \"Let's breathe.\" 4. Praise the breathing. First: her early signs, from the assessment."],[11.7,"N","First, find the early signs that come before her aggression during work. They come from the assessment."],[18.8,"O","Check: she can imitate it when calm."],[19.1,"N","Check that she can imitate the breathing when she is calm."],[22.8,"O","Now: 1. Teach it when calm."],[23.4,"N","Teach it when she is calm. Model slow belly breathing: in through the nose, and out through the mouth."],[31.3,"O","Now: 1. Teach it when calm. In through the nose, out through the mouth."],[37.8,"O","Now: 1. Teach it when calm."],[38.1,"N","Then have her practice, and praise it."],[43.4,"Teacher","Nice slow breathing."],[48.0,"O","Now: 2. Watch for the first signs."],[48.3,"N","During work, watch for her early signs."],[52.0,"O","Now: 2. Watch for the first signs. A first sign: tense, quick breathing."],[58.6,"O","Now: 3. At a sign: \"Let's breathe.\""],[58.9,"N","At a sign, prompt the breathing."],[63.7,"Teacher","Let's breathe."],[68.5,"O","Now: 4. Praise the breathing."],[68.8,"N","And praise her for breathing."],[73.5,"Teacher","Good breathing."],[77.9,"O","No drop: add DRO, or FCT for a break."],[78.2,"N","This card uses no extinction. If her aggression does not fall, move to a function-based part: DRO, or FCT for a break."],[89.2,"O","In the study, breathing prompted at the first signs reduced aggression for one of the three participants."],[89.2,"O","The other two needed DRO, or FCT for a break, as well."],[89.5,"N","In the study, breathing prompted at the first signs reduced aggression for one of the three participants. The other two needed DRO, or FCT for a break, as well."],[102.2,"O","This card uses no extinction. Record aggression per minute, and prompted breathing per session, on Form MT-1."],[102.2,"O","A coping skill does not replace a function-based plan."],[102.5,"N","This card uses no extinction. Record aggression per minute, and prompted breathing per session, on Form MT-1. A coping skill does not replace a function-based plan."],[115.4,"O","The evidence is three individuals with autism or Angelman syndrome, seen in a clinic, with aggression maintained by escape from work."],[115.4,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[115.7,"N","The evidence is three individuals with autism or Angelman syndrome, seen in a clinic, with aggression maintained by escape from work. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "calm_muscle": [[0.0,"O","Teach the sequence in a quiet room when she is calm. Then prompt and praise it in the setting that is hard for her"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Gardner, A. W., Weddle, S. A., Yaggie, M. J., Anguiano, C., Davidson, A., & Tureaud, C. (2026). Assessment and treatment of anxious avoidant responses to auditory stimuli via physiological and behavioral methodologies. Behavioral Interventions, 41(4), e70139."],[0.6,"N","Muscle relaxation: tense, and release. A simulated example of the TD-1 card after Gardner and colleagues, 2026."],[12.0,"O","Muscle relaxation: 1. A quiet room, when calm. 2. Tense, then release. 3. Large muscles, then small. 4. Then prompt about every 2 min. Now: 1. A quiet room, when calm."],[12.3,"N","In the study, a student was upset by the sounds of people eating. Teach relaxation in a quiet room, away from the sounds that upset her, and only when she is calm. The study used three sessions of about one hour."],[27.8,"O","Now: 2. Tense, then release."],[28.4,"N","Model it first: tense a muscle group, hold, and then release."],[40.6,"O","Now: 3. Large muscles, then small."],[40.9,"N","Then have her do it. Start with the large muscle groups: the arms, and the legs."],[46.7,"O","Now: 3. Large muscles, then small. Large first: arms, legs."],[49.9,"Teacher","Now let go."],[53.3,"O","Now: 3. Large muscles, then small. Then small: face, hands."],[53.6,"N","Then move to the smaller ones: the face, and the hands."],[61.5,"Teacher","Nice relaxing."],[64.8,"O","Prompt as needed; praise each cycle."],[65.7,"N","Prompt only as needed, and praise each completed cycle of tensing and releasing."],[71.8,"O","Learned: alone, 2 sessions in a row."],[72.1,"N","Count the skill as learned when she completes the whole sequence on her own, with few verbal prompts, in two sessions in a row."],[86.8,"O","Now: 4. Then prompt about every 2 min."],[87.1,"N","Then practice in the room where the sounds will be, at first with no one eating. Prompt, and praise, relaxing about every two minutes. Then keep doing so in each ten-minute session with someone eating nearby."],[101.8,"O","Now: 4. Then prompt about every 2 min. The person eating is not shown."],[103.0,"Teacher","Relax your shoulders."],[108.4,"O","Record: 6-second intervals, each session."],[109.3,"N","Record the behavior in six-second intervals across each session."],[114.0,"O","In the study, a student was upset by the sounds of people eating. Relaxation was combined with highly preferred activities while someone ate nearby."],[114.0,"O","Together they went with lower arousal, and less of the behavior, than low-preferred items did."],[114.3,"N","In the study, relaxation was combined with highly preferred activities while someone ate nearby. Together, they went with lower arousal, and less of the behavior, than low-preferred items did."],[127.5,"O","Teach relaxation only when the student is calm. Plan the exposure with the family, and with any clinician the family uses."],[127.5,"O","Keep a way for the student to ask to leave."],[127.8,"N","Teach relaxation only when the student is calm. Plan the exposure with the family, and with any clinician the family uses. And keep a way for the student to ask to leave."],[140.1,"O","The evidence is one student, with no clear experimental control. Relaxation was not separated from the preferred activities, and use without prompts was not measured."],[140.1,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[140.4,"N","The evidence is one student, with no clear experimental control. Relaxation was not separated from the preferred activities, and use without prompts was not measured. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]],
 "calm_plan": [[0.0,"O","Notice the signs, go to the calm-down area, breathe and relax: taught by role play, with a practice log"],[0.0,"O","TD-1 protocol bank · Zencius, A., & Wesolowski, M. D. (1990). Brief report: Using stress management to decrease inappropriate behavior in a brain injured adult. Behavioral Residential Treatment, 5(1), 61–64."],[0.6,"N","A three-step calm-down plan, that the student starts herself. A simulated example of the TD-1 card after Zencius and Wesolowski, 1990."],[11.9,"O","The three steps: 1. Notice your own signs. 2. Go to the calm-down area. 3. Breathe, then tense and relax. Now: 1. Notice your own signs."],[12.2,"N","Help her list her own early signs of stress, such as tight muscles, or the start of an outburst."],[18.9,"O","Now: 1. Notice your own signs. Her own list of early signs."],[25.5,"O","Now: 2. Go to the calm-down area."],[25.8,"N","Agree on a calm-down area in each setting, that she may go to without asking."],[33.5,"Teacher","You can go there without asking."],[38.1,"O","Model and role-play all three."],[38.4,"N","Model, and role-play, the three steps: notice the signs, walk to the calm-down area, then breathe deeply, and tense and relax muscle groups."],[50.2,"O","Now: 2. Go to the calm-down area. Role play: practice going."],[50.5,"N","During role plays, tell her when a situation is stressful, and have her practice going to the area."],[57.8,"Teacher","This is a stressful moment."],[63.9,"O","Now: 2. Go to the calm-down area. Safe, and within staff view."],[64.2,"N","Later, she goes there on her own, without asking. The area must be safe, and within staff view."],[71.8,"O","Now: 3. Breathe, then tense and relax."],[72.1,"N","There, she breathes deeply, and tenses and relaxes her muscles."],[82.5,"O","Log: why, how long, where, calmer?"],[82.8,"N","Teach in two twenty-five minute sessions a week, one in each setting, for four weeks. Assign practice three times a week, and any time she feels anxious. She records each practice in a log: why, how long, where, and whether she felt calmer."],[100.2,"O","Weekly: about 10 minutes together."],[100.5,"N","Once a week, collect the log, hand out a new one, and spend about ten minutes answering her questions."],[110.2,"Teacher","Any questions about your practice?"],[114.2,"O","In the study, running away fell from a mean of about 2.2 times a week to 0.1 once training began, and was at zero at a 6-week follow-up."],[114.2,"O","Training was two 25-minute sessions a week for four weeks, one in each setting."],[114.5,"N","In the study, running away fell from a mean of about two times a week, to zero point one once training began, and was at zero at a six-week follow-up."],[125.5,"O","A calm-down area the student goes to alone must be safe and within staff view, so that leaving for it does not become unsupervised running away."],[125.5,"O","Check what running away achieves for the student before relying on this alone."],[125.8,"N","A calm-down area that the student goes to alone must be safe, and within staff view, so that leaving for it does not become unsupervised running away. Check what running away achieves for the student, before relying on this alone."],[141.6,"O","The evidence is one adult with a brain injury and good language skills, in a brief report with no reversal. The extra staff attention around practice may have helped."],[141.6,"O","This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."],[141.9,"N","The evidence is one adult with a brain injury, and good language skills, in a brief report with no reversal. The extra staff attention around practice may have helped. This is a simulated example. It shows the sequence, not a real session."]]
};
